WEBVTT

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:07.836
Welcome to our School Leadership
Professional Learning Workshop.

00:00:07.836 --> 00:00:11.609
Today we're going to explore Autistic
experience and what it

00:00:11.609 --> 00:00:14.629
means for access, belonging and
partnership across a school.

00:00:14.629 --> 00:00:17.770
Keep the students you work with at the
centre of the day.

00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:21.911
We'll build our understanding, look
closely at everyday school

00:00:21.911 --> 00:00:25.094
routines and consider what we can change
in the environment, the

00:00:25.094 --> 00:00:26.909
task and the support we offer.

00:00:26.909 --> 00:00:30.765
Our starting point is curiosity. We are
here to understand

00:00:30.765 --> 00:00:33.192
students and make participation more
workable.

00:00:33.396 --> 00:00:37.129
We value students as they are and support
their participation

00:00:37.129 --> 00:00:38.007
and belonging.

00:00:38.007 --> 00:00:41.781
You don't need to arrive with all the
answers. Bring your

00:00:41.781 --> 00:00:44.555
questions, your experience and a
willingness to look again.

00:00:44.555 --> 00:00:48.329
As leaders you shape the conditions in
which that understanding

00:00:48.329 --> 00:00:50.022
becomes everyday practice.

00:00:50.022 --> 00:00:53.755
We will keep different students in view as
we look at school

00:00:53.755 --> 00:00:56.489
routines, culture, resourcing and
partnership.

00:00:58.681 --> 00:01:03.603
I acknowledge the Traditional Custodians
of the lands on which we are learning

00:01:03.604 --> 00:01:08.718
today, and pay my respects to Elders past
and present. I recognise the continuing

00:01:08.719 --> 00:01:13.258
connections of Aboriginal and Torres
Strait Islander peoples to Country,

00:01:13.259 --> 00:01:18.053
culture and community. I extend that
respect to Aboriginal and Torres Strait

00:01:18.054 --> 00:01:21.249
Islander people taking part in this
learning today.

00:01:24.280 --> 00:01:27.340
Our Statement of Reconciliation is in your
workbook for you to read.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:30.480
For the sake of this demonstration, I'll
read it aloud.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:34.440
We at Positive Partnerships value and
recognise the diversity of

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:37.880
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander
cultures and heritage

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:42.080
and their proud part in Australia’s
national identity.

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:46.090
We acknowledge and continue to learn about
Australia’s colonial

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:51.200
past and the historic and ongoing impacts
on Australia’s First Nations people.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:55.830
We believe in and strongly support the
Truth-telling of

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:57.700
Australia’s colonial history

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:01.410
and in moving forward as a nation we must
encourage open,

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:05.840
honest dialogue and build relationships
built on respect and trust.

00:02:08.241 --> 00:02:12.633
First Nations people are advised that this
workshop may contain images and

00:02:12.634 --> 00:02:15.159
voices of people who are no longer with
us.

00:02:17.161 --> 00:02:22.461
Everyone is welcome here, and you are
welcome to be authentically yourself. Use

00:02:22.462 --> 00:02:27.626
the strategies that help you take part.
You can move, change position, write,

00:02:27.627 --> 00:02:32.451
speak, listen or pass. If there is
something the delivery team can do to

00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:37.752
make participation more accessible, let us
know. Let's begin by getting to know

00:02:37.753 --> 00:02:42.985
the people around us. At your table, share
your first name, your role, and one

00:02:42.986 --> 00:02:47.743
hope you have for today. Keep the
introduction brief so everyone has an

00:02:47.744 --> 00:02:53.179
opportunity. You're also welcome simply to
listen. There is no need to disclose a

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:58.345
diagnosis or share private information
about yourself, a student or a school.

00:02:58.346 --> 00:03:03.510
What would make this learning useful in
your work? Take a moment to introduce

00:03:03.511 --> 00:03:04.528
yourselves now.

00:03:12.561 --> 00:03:17.016
Let's come back together. Keep that hope
in mind as we go. There will be

00:03:17.017 --> 00:03:22.100
opportunities to connect the content with
sensory experience, thinking, the school

00:03:22.101 --> 00:03:26.556
day, learning design and partnership. Your
questions are useful starting

00:03:26.557 --> 00:03:29.881
points, even when they don't have an
immediate answer.

00:03:31.401 --> 00:03:35.164
Before we get into the content, take a
moment to check the practical

00:03:35.165 --> 00:03:39.714
arrangements for your setting: the exits,
bathroom facilities, tea and coffee, and

00:03:39.715 --> 00:03:44.096
any quiet or lower-sensory space that is
available. Keep your Participant Guide

00:03:44.097 --> 00:03:48.477
and something to write with nearby. We'll
use the guide throughout the day, and

00:03:48.478 --> 00:03:52.634
there will be time to provide feedback
through the evaluation. You can also

00:03:52.635 --> 00:03:56.903
write questions down if speaking to the
group isn't your preferred way to ask

00:03:56.904 --> 00:04:01.285
them. Please be considerate with phone
sounds and calls, and follow the venue's

00:04:01.286 --> 00:04:05.667
arrangements for no smoking or vaping.
Attend to your own needs as we work. You

00:04:05.668 --> 00:04:09.711
don't need to wait for a scheduled break
to use a strategy that helps you

00:04:09.712 --> 00:04:13.473
participate. Confirm any local
arrangements with your delivery team.

00:04:15.001 --> 00:04:19.774
Turn to page four of your Participant
Guide. Bring two or three students to

00:04:19.775 --> 00:04:24.741
mind. Choose students whose experiences
differ in some way: perhaps their age,

00:04:24.742 --> 00:04:29.709
how they communicate, or what different
parts of school are like for them. Use

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:34.225
initials or a role description in your
notes. We won't need identifying

00:04:34.226 --> 00:04:39.321
information. Begin with the whole person.
What interests them? What strengths do

00:04:39.322 --> 00:04:44.031
you notice? How do they connect with
people or with the things they enjoy?

00:04:44.032 --> 00:04:48.998
Then ask yourself: what am I here to
understand more deeply about their school

00:04:48.999 --> 00:04:53.708
experience? Keep that as an open question,
rather than deciding the answer

00:04:53.709 --> 00:04:58.353
now. This is private thinking first.
Sharing is optional. We'll return to

00:04:58.354 --> 00:05:03.256
these same students when we map the school
day, consider learning design, and

00:05:03.257 --> 00:05:08.352
reflect on what to carry forward. Give
yourself a moment to choose your students

00:05:08.353 --> 00:05:09.706
and make a few notes.

00:05:17.721 --> 00:05:22.443
Keep those notes available. You don't need
a complete picture yet. The gaps in

00:05:22.444 --> 00:05:26.798
what we know are part of the work. As we
move through the workshop, keep

00:05:26.799 --> 00:05:31.214
distinguishing what a student has
communicated, what you've observed, and

00:05:31.215 --> 00:05:33.972
what you'd still like to understand with
them.

00:05:35.481 --> 00:05:40.237
We're going to begin with student voices.
Listen to each speaker's own account, and

00:05:40.238 --> 00:05:44.821
allow it to stand on its own terms. You
don't need to diagnose, compare speakers

00:05:44.822 --> 00:05:49.114
with students you know, or reduce the
video to one lesson. Just notice what

00:05:49.115 --> 00:05:53.466
stays with you, and what you want to
understand more deeply. We'll have time

00:05:53.467 --> 00:05:55.030
to reflect after the video.

00:08:17.441 --> 00:08:21.341
Take a moment before moving into
discussion. Keep the speakers' own words

00:08:21.342 --> 00:08:23.940
and experiences at the centre of your
reflection.

00:08:26.961 --> 00:08:31.850
Stay on page four of your Participant
Guide. What did you notice? What has

00:08:31.851 --> 00:08:36.874
stayed with you? What would you like to
understand more deeply? Begin with a

00:08:36.875 --> 00:08:42.166
little private writing. Anchor your notes
in what the speakers said, rather than

00:08:42.167 --> 00:08:46.990
making an interpretation from their
appearance or behaviour. Then, if you

00:08:46.991 --> 00:08:51.947
wish, share one reflection with someone
nearby. Try to leave each speaker's

00:08:51.948 --> 00:08:57.172
account intact. We don't need to reach
agreement about a single message, and we

00:08:57.173 --> 00:09:02.062
don't need to compare a speaker with one
particular child. Make room for a

00:09:02.063 --> 00:09:07.019
question that remains open as well as
something that feels clear. Take some

00:09:07.020 --> 00:09:08.895
time with those prompts now.

00:09:16.921 --> 00:09:21.737
Let's return. Keep one question from that
reflection where you can see it. We will

00:09:21.738 --> 00:09:25.900
come back to sensory experience, thinking,
communication and the school

00:09:25.901 --> 00:09:30.420
day. Some questions may need further
conversation through the program or your

00:09:30.421 --> 00:09:34.821
local channels. Listening carefully
includes recognising what we still need

00:09:34.822 --> 00:09:35.356
to learn.

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:41.528
Let's take a moment with the language on
this slide, because it gives us a shared

00:09:41.528 --> 00:09:45.768
starting point. Neurodiversity is the
whole picture. It includes everyone.

00:09:45.768 --> 00:09:50.474
Neurotypical and neurodivergent describe
brain patterns within that diversity, and

00:09:50.474 --> 00:09:54.831
autism is one expression of it. Here,
neurotypical describes the more common

00:09:54.831 --> 00:09:59.304
brain patterns. Neurodivergent describes
brain patterns that differ from those

00:09:59.304 --> 00:10:03.720
most common patterns. So both sit within
the wider picture of neurodiversity.

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:08.077
Keeping those ideas clear helps us use the
language consistently through the

00:10:08.077 --> 00:10:12.550
day. Now, hold in mind the students you
were thinking about earlier. The words

00:10:12.550 --> 00:10:17.081
can help us ask better questions about
their experience, but they won't tell us

00:10:17.081 --> 00:10:21.090
everything about any one of them. We still
need to know that student's

00:10:21.090 --> 00:10:25.563
interests, their ways of communicating,
and what different parts of school are

00:10:25.563 --> 00:10:29.978
like for them. We'll also respect the
language people use for themselves. The

00:10:29.978 --> 00:10:34.394
purpose here is to support dignity and
curiosity. As we go, keep asking: does

00:10:34.394 --> 00:10:37.761
this language help me understand the
person in front of me?

00:10:39.281 --> 00:10:44.038
That brings us to what it means to be
neurodiversity affirming. The starting

00:10:44.039 --> 00:10:48.478
point is that differences are not
deficits. There are different ways of

00:10:48.479 --> 00:10:53.426
knowing, doing, being and belonging, and
everyone has strengths and experiences

00:10:53.427 --> 00:10:58.184
challenges. That means allowing for
different ways of thinking, learning and

00:10:58.185 --> 00:11:03.005
taking part. When we look at belonging,
for instance, we can stay open to the

00:11:03.006 --> 00:11:08.017
ways a student experiences connection,
rather than expecting it to look the same

00:11:08.018 --> 00:11:12.711
for everyone. In a school, it's easy for
the things that need our immediate

00:11:12.712 --> 00:11:17.659
attention to fill the whole picture. A
difficult transition. A task that hasn't

00:11:17.660 --> 00:11:22.798
started. A moment when joining the group
has become too much. Those things matter.

00:11:22.799 --> 00:11:27.746
We need to understand them and provide
support. And we need to keep the rest of

00:11:27.747 --> 00:11:32.377
the person visible. What interests them?
What brings them joy? How do they

00:11:32.378 --> 00:11:36.817
connect? What can they do that we might
miss if we're only watching for

00:11:36.818 --> 00:11:41.639
difficulty? Take one of your students now.
What becomes more visible when you

00:11:41.640 --> 00:11:46.777
include their strengths and interests in
the picture? Just hold that thought for a

00:11:46.778 --> 00:11:47.220
moment.

00:11:55.240 --> 00:12:00.481
A strengths-based approach also takes
difficulty seriously. A student can have

00:12:00.482 --> 00:12:05.928
real strengths and still need substantial
support. Their capacity can change with

00:12:05.929 --> 00:12:11.511
the task, the environment and the day. So
our language needs to make room for both.

00:12:11.512 --> 00:12:16.276
We can describe what's becoming difficult,
notice what the situation is

00:12:16.277 --> 00:12:21.519
asking of the student, and consider what
support would help. We begin with the

00:12:21.520 --> 00:12:24.310
person, and we stay curious about access.

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:30.499
The social model helps us put that
curiosity to work. On this slide, we're

00:12:30.500 --> 00:12:35.606
using an environment-first lens. We look
for barriers in the place, the task, the

00:12:35.607 --> 00:12:40.458
routine and the expectations. Then we ask
what can change around the student.

00:12:40.459 --> 00:12:45.373
Here's an imaginary classroom example. A
class is gathering on the floor for a

00:12:45.374 --> 00:12:50.288
story. The usual expectation is that
everyone sits close together, in the same

00:12:50.289 --> 00:12:55.459
space, for the whole reading. One student
finds that arrangement difficult. Before

00:12:55.460 --> 00:13:00.183
deciding what the student needs to do
differently, we can look at what this

00:13:00.184 --> 00:13:05.354
routine asks of them. How crowded is the
space? What choices are there about where

00:13:05.355 --> 00:13:09.950
to sit? Is sitting in that particular
place necessary to take part in the

00:13:09.951 --> 00:13:15.121
story? We might explore another place to
sit, or a different way of joining. Those

00:13:15.122 --> 00:13:20.356
are possibilities to investigate with the
student. We haven't established what will

00:13:20.357 --> 00:13:25.271
help just by coming up with an option. The
next two steps on the slide matter:

00:13:25.272 --> 00:13:30.378
include the student's communication and
experience, and review whether the change

00:13:30.379 --> 00:13:35.485
makes participation more workable. What
can the student tell or show us about the

00:13:35.486 --> 00:13:40.400
change? Is it helping them take part?
Changing an environment won't make every

00:13:40.401 --> 00:13:45.060
difficulty disappear. It gives us
somewhere useful to begin, with barriers

00:13:45.061 --> 00:13:48.316
and supports that we can notice, discuss
and review.

00:13:49.841 --> 00:13:54.769
Let's widen that question to school
itself. You can use the space on page

00:13:54.770 --> 00:14:00.040
five of your Participant Guide as you
think. What is school for? Who is school

00:14:00.041 --> 00:14:04.832
for? How do students learn? And whose ways
of communicating, moving and

00:14:04.833 --> 00:14:10.172
participating are treated as expected?
These are big questions. There isn't one

00:14:10.173 --> 00:14:15.580
correct answer we're trying to arrive at.
They're an invitation to look again at

00:14:15.581 --> 00:14:20.646
familiar routines and the assumptions
inside them. Think about a routine in

00:14:20.647 --> 00:14:25.849
your school: perhaps arriving in the
morning, joining a group or showing what

00:14:25.850 --> 00:14:30.983
you've learnt. What does that routine seem
to assume about how students will

00:14:30.984 --> 00:14:35.981
communicate or participate? What do we
notice and encourage? What might be

00:14:35.982 --> 00:14:41.115
harder to see? This is a chance to examine
the routine with curiosity. Every

00:14:41.116 --> 00:14:46.318
school has patterns that have become
familiar. Looking at them again can open

00:14:46.319 --> 00:14:51.589
up choices. We can value the care and
effort that educators bring, while still

00:14:51.590 --> 00:14:56.518
asking whether a familiar expectation is
helping every student access the

00:14:56.519 --> 00:15:01.379
learning. For a moment, work quietly on
your own. Choose one of the four

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:06.445
questions that catches your attention, and
jot down a thought or a question

00:15:06.446 --> 00:15:07.950
you'd like to explore.

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:20.653
Hold on to the question you've chosen. Our
assumptions influence what we notice,

00:15:20.654 --> 00:15:25.049
what we reward and what we think needs to
change. We'll now take that wider

00:15:25.050 --> 00:15:29.624
reflection into one specific routine, so
the question becomes something we can

00:15:29.625 --> 00:15:30.634
examine together.

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:37.382
Now turn to a small group near you. To
begin, take the question about whose ways

00:15:37.383 --> 00:15:41.746
of communicating, moving and participating
are treated as expected.

00:15:41.747 --> 00:15:46.770
Choose one familiar routine or decision to
explore together. Keep it specific

00:15:46.771 --> 00:15:51.927
enough to picture. You might choose how
children join a whole-class discussion,

00:15:51.928 --> 00:15:56.886
for example. Talk about the routine and
the system, without naming students,

00:15:56.887 --> 00:16:02.175
colleagues or schools. Then work through
the prompts on screen. Which assumptions

00:16:02.176 --> 00:16:06.935
shape that routine? Who can access it
easily? Where might the environment

00:16:06.936 --> 00:16:11.827
create a barrier? How could curiosity
change the next response? If you find

00:16:11.828 --> 00:16:16.521
yourselves focusing mainly on what a
student does, come back to what the

00:16:16.522 --> 00:16:21.017
environment, task or expectation is
contributing. Before we come back

00:16:21.018 --> 00:16:25.777
together, choose one concise observation
to share, and one example of how

00:16:25.778 --> 00:16:30.604
curiosity could change your next response.
Keep the prompts in view as you

00:16:30.605 --> 00:16:31.926
talk. You can begin.

00:16:39.960 --> 00:16:44.726
Let's come back together. Finish the
thought you're on, and keep your notes.

00:16:44.727 --> 00:16:49.493
If you're sharing with the group, offer
one concise observation: one example

00:16:49.494 --> 00:16:54.514
where curiosity could change your next
response. Keep the example focused on the

00:16:54.515 --> 00:16:59.535
routine, the task or the environment. As
we close, keep that question about your

00:16:59.536 --> 00:17:04.302
next response with you. The shift we're
practising is from assumptions about

00:17:04.303 --> 00:17:08.878
students towards questions about access,
environment and learning design.

00:17:08.879 --> 00:17:13.518
Curiosity gives us time to look at the
task and the setting before placing

00:17:13.519 --> 00:17:18.539
another demand on a student. It helps us
identify something to explore, with the

00:17:18.540 --> 00:17:23.560
student's experience still at the centre.
Keep your example available as we move

00:17:23.561 --> 00:17:23.751
on.

00:17:25.280 --> 00:17:30.742
The Disability Standards for Education
2005 are part of the context for our work

00:17:30.743 --> 00:17:36.066
on access and adjustments. There are three
Australian Government Department of

00:17:36.067 --> 00:17:40.975
Education resources to be aware of
alongside this workshop. The first is

00:17:40.976 --> 00:17:46.507
Getting started. The second is Advocating
with and for your child: A workbook for

00:17:46.508 --> 00:17:51.831
parents and carers. That workbook is
written for families, so it is useful for

00:17:51.832 --> 00:17:57.363
educators to know what parents and carers
may be reading and working through. The

00:17:57.364 --> 00:18:02.342
third resource is Milestones and
transitions. The links are listed in the

00:18:02.343 --> 00:18:07.943
facilitator resources. Use the published
materials to follow up, and take detailed

00:18:07.944 --> 00:18:13.543
or school-specific questions through the
appropriate program or local channel. For

00:18:13.544 --> 00:18:18.591
our work today, keep the connection with
access in view: understanding the

00:18:18.592 --> 00:18:23.708
student, looking closely at the demands of
the setting, and considering the

00:18:23.709 --> 00:18:27.926
adjustments and support that make
participation more workable.

00:18:29.440 --> 00:18:34.547
Autism can be understood as difference and
as a dynamic disability. Holding both

00:18:34.548 --> 00:18:39.460
ideas matters. Recognising difference and
strengths does not mean overlooking

00:18:39.461 --> 00:18:44.310
difficulty or the need for substantial
support. Access and support needs can

00:18:44.311 --> 00:18:49.353
change with the task, the environment,
relationships and the capacity available

00:18:49.354 --> 00:18:54.590
at that time. Something a student can do
in one setting, or on one day, may not be

00:18:54.591 --> 00:18:59.697
available in another. We need to look at
what is happening around the student as

00:18:59.698 --> 00:19:04.934
well as what the task is asking of them.
That takes us away from a fixed judgement

00:19:04.935 --> 00:19:09.783
about what someone can always do. It
invites a more useful question: what is

00:19:09.784 --> 00:19:14.632
making participation possible here, and
what support is needed now? Keep the

00:19:14.633 --> 00:19:19.481
individual in view. There isn't one
pattern that will explain every Autistic

00:19:19.482 --> 00:19:24.201
student's experience. Strengths,
difficulties and fluctuating capacity all

00:19:24.202 --> 00:19:29.115
belong in the picture we build with the
student and the people who know them.

00:19:30.641 --> 00:19:35.593
Here is where the day is taking us. We'll
develop a neurodiversity affirming

00:19:35.594 --> 00:19:40.413
approach, deepen our understanding of
Autistic experience, notice how that

00:19:40.414 --> 00:19:45.564
experience meets the school day, consider
accessible learning environments, and

00:19:45.565 --> 00:19:50.451
work in partnership. The first two
sessions build our shared understanding.

00:19:50.452 --> 00:19:55.667
Then we'll bring that understanding into
the experience of a school day and into

00:19:55.668 --> 00:20:00.818
practical choices about school systems,
design and support. Belonging, capacity

00:20:00.819 --> 00:20:05.837
and joy stay with us throughout. The
workshop schedule allows for morning tea

00:20:05.838 --> 00:20:11.186
from ten forty-five to eleven, and lunch
from twelve thirty to one. Treat those as

00:20:11.187 --> 00:20:16.072
the planned timings and follow the
arrangements for your session. Before we

00:20:16.073 --> 00:20:21.223
move on, turn to My starting point on page
five of your Participant Guide. Make

00:20:21.224 --> 00:20:26.572
a brief note of where you are beginning: a
question, a hope or an idea you want to

00:20:26.573 --> 00:20:31.723
examine. We will return to this in our
final reflection. Take a moment to write

00:20:31.724 --> 00:20:31.987
now.

00:20:40.000 --> 00:20:44.422
Keep that starting point. You don't have
to resolve it yet. Next we'll explore

00:20:44.423 --> 00:20:49.018
sensory experience, thinking and internal
signals, using the students you have in

00:20:49.019 --> 00:20:52.120
mind to connect the ideas with your own
school context.

00:20:58.641 --> 00:21:03.654
Let's return to the students you brought
to mind. In this session we'll explore

00:21:03.655 --> 00:21:08.474
sensory processing, Autistic thinking and
internal signals. You can find the

00:21:08.475 --> 00:21:13.038
session summary on page six of your
Participant Guide. We are building a

00:21:13.039 --> 00:21:17.923
fuller picture of each student. These
ideas give us areas for curiosity; they

00:21:17.924 --> 00:21:22.680
are not a checklist for deciding what a
student must be like. Keep noticing

00:21:22.681 --> 00:21:27.758
strengths and interests, the environment,
the demands of a task, and the support

00:21:27.759 --> 00:21:32.707
that may help. We'll look at these areas
separately so we can understand them,

00:21:32.708 --> 00:21:37.528
then begin putting them together again. In
the next session we'll carry that

00:21:37.529 --> 00:21:42.284
understanding into communication,
connection and the everyday experience of

00:21:42.285 --> 00:21:45.497
school, including the conditions leaders
can shape.

00:21:47.000 --> 00:21:51.794
We'll start with sensory experience: how
the world arrives. Before we ask a

00:21:51.795 --> 00:21:56.719
student to join a conversation, begin a
task or make sense of an instruction,

00:21:56.720 --> 00:22:01.709
there is already a sensory environment
around them. That environment can bring

00:22:01.710 --> 00:22:06.570
pleasure, interest and connection, as well
as difficulty. Keep both in view.

00:22:06.571 --> 00:22:11.365
We're interested in sensory joy and
authentic expression alongside overload

00:22:11.366 --> 00:22:16.420
and barriers. As we work, remember that an
observation can open a question, but

00:22:16.421 --> 00:22:21.215
it cannot tell us everything about someone
else's internal experience. Stay

00:22:21.216 --> 00:22:23.872
curious about the person and the context.

00:22:25.400 --> 00:22:29.933
What do you understand by sensory
processing? Before we settle on a shared

00:22:29.934 --> 00:22:34.903
definition, take a moment to put it into
your own words. Think about what happens

00:22:34.904 --> 00:22:39.934
between information arriving through the
senses and the response you might notice.

00:22:47.960 --> 00:22:53.572
For this workshop, sensory processing is
the way the brain receives, processes and

00:22:53.573 --> 00:22:59.047
responds to information from the senses.
There are three parts to hold together:

00:22:59.048 --> 00:23:04.106
receiving information, making sense of it,
and responding. We'll look more

00:23:04.107 --> 00:23:09.235
closely at that sequence in a moment.
First, let's widen the picture of the

00:23:09.236 --> 00:23:10.482
senses themselves.

00:23:12.000 --> 00:23:17.145
Many of us learnt five senses: touch,
vision, taste, hearing and smell. This

00:23:17.146 --> 00:23:22.360
workshop uses eight. The other three are
proprioception, vestibular sense and

00:23:22.361 --> 00:23:27.917
interoception. Touch includes information
about texture, pressure and temperature.

00:23:27.918 --> 00:23:32.652
Vision gives information about things such
as colour, shape, depth and

00:23:32.653 --> 00:23:38.072
position. Gustatory and oral experience
includes taste, texture, temperature and

00:23:38.073 --> 00:23:42.944
the sensation of chewing. Hearing includes
pitch, loudness, distance and

00:23:42.945 --> 00:23:47.335
familiar sounds. Smell can also be closely
connected with memory.

00:23:47.336 --> 00:23:52.755
Proprioception gives us information about
where our body is in space, drawing on

00:23:52.756 --> 00:23:57.901
signals from our muscles and joints.
Pressure or movement may be something a

00:23:57.902 --> 00:24:02.842
student seeks for that input. The
vestibular sense relates to balance and

00:24:02.843 --> 00:24:07.713
movement, including the speed and
direction of head movement. Rocking or

00:24:07.714 --> 00:24:13.134
spinning may serve a regulating purpose
for some students. Interoception relates

00:24:13.135 --> 00:24:18.485
to signals from inside the body. We'll
return to it later in the session. These

00:24:18.486 --> 00:24:23.906
senses work together. A single classroom
experience can involve several at once.

00:24:23.907 --> 00:24:28.777
The three less familiar senses can be
especially useful to consider when

00:24:28.778 --> 00:24:33.855
something about a student's experience
isn't yet clear to us. Keep these as

00:24:33.856 --> 00:24:39.001
possibilities to explore, rather than
explanations we can assign by watching

00:24:39.002 --> 00:24:44.010
alone. Students will differ. There is also
an interoception webinar in the

00:24:44.011 --> 00:24:47.850
Positive Partnerships resources if you
want to follow up.

00:24:49.360 --> 00:24:53.558
Here is the sequence again: sensory
information, processing, response.

00:24:53.559 --> 00:24:57.574
Imagine you're wearing a jumper and
becoming hot. Information about

00:24:57.575 --> 00:25:02.381
temperature arrives. You make sense of
that information, including what it means

00:25:02.382 --> 00:25:07.127
in relation to your experience, and you
might respond by taking the jumper off.

00:25:07.128 --> 00:25:11.265
In a classroom, this processing helps us
keep track of our bodies and

00:25:11.266 --> 00:25:15.890
surroundings, notice what matters and
direct attention. It is part of safety,

00:25:15.891 --> 00:25:20.819
learning and engagement. There is a great
deal happening before a response becomes

00:25:20.820 --> 00:25:25.626
visible to someone else. That is why we
need to look beyond the behaviour we can

00:25:25.627 --> 00:25:30.372
see. Sensory processing is not simply a
deliberate choice a student makes about

00:25:30.373 --> 00:25:34.571
how the world affects them. When we notice
a response, we can ask what

00:25:34.572 --> 00:25:39.074
information is arriving, what the
environment is asking of the student, and

00:25:39.075 --> 00:25:41.325
what we need to understand with them.

00:25:42.840 --> 00:25:47.832
We're going to watch two short clips. The
first is an animation that explores

00:25:47.833 --> 00:25:52.891
sensory overwhelm through images and
sound. The next offers a personal account

00:25:52.892 --> 00:25:57.687
of sensory joy. Both belong in our
understanding of sensory experience. As

00:25:57.688 --> 00:26:02.351
you watch the animation, notice what it
draws your attention to. You may

00:26:02.352 --> 00:26:07.475
recognise something, or you may encounter
a portrayal that is unfamiliar. Leave

00:26:07.476 --> 00:26:12.468
room for either response, and remember
that no single portrayal can represent

00:26:12.469 --> 00:26:17.461
every Autistic person's experience. We'll
leave a quiet moment after the clip

00:26:17.462 --> 00:26:18.644
before continuing.

00:28:45.880 --> 00:28:50.841
Take a moment with what you've seen and
heard. The animation gives us a way to

00:28:50.842 --> 00:28:55.738
consider sensory overwhelm, while each
person's experience remains their own.

00:28:55.739 --> 00:29:00.507
Keep any questions it raises with you.
We'll now make room for sensory joy.

00:29:03.520 --> 00:29:09.060
In this next clip, we'll hear a personal
account of sensory joy. As you listen,

00:29:09.061 --> 00:29:14.246
consider what becomes visible when we
include pleasure, connection and the

00:29:14.247 --> 00:29:17.869
richness of sensory experience in our
understanding.

00:30:07.360 --> 00:30:13.117
Sensory experience can include intensity,
depth and pleasure as well as difficulty.

00:30:13.118 --> 00:30:18.665
The same person can experience both joy
and overwhelm. For example, music may be

00:30:18.666 --> 00:30:23.792
a source of enjoyment for someone, while a
different sound or situation is

00:30:23.793 --> 00:30:28.637
difficult. That is an illustration, rather
than a conclusion about any

00:30:28.638 --> 00:30:33.904
individual speaker. Keep asking what
brings this particular student joy, and

00:30:33.905 --> 00:30:36.151
how school can make room for it.

00:30:37.680 --> 00:30:42.735
Sensory joy and stimming belong in this
conversation. Stimming can be part of

00:30:42.736 --> 00:30:47.525
regulation, pleasure, connection and
authentic expression. Our task is to

00:30:47.526 --> 00:30:52.913
understand what it means for the student,
rather than treating it as disruption by

00:30:52.914 --> 00:30:57.104
default. Notice what brings joy. Recognise
the student's ways of

00:30:57.105 --> 00:31:02.160
regulating and expressing themselves.
Avoid making harmless stimming a target

00:31:02.161 --> 00:31:06.950
simply because it looks different from
what is expected. And ask what the

00:31:06.951 --> 00:31:11.873
student would like other people to
understand. Think about one student now.

00:31:11.874 --> 00:31:16.862
Where is sensory joy visible in their
school day? What opportunities do they

00:31:16.863 --> 00:31:21.785
have to enjoy, explore or regulate in ways
that work for them? Make a brief

00:31:21.786 --> 00:31:25.976
note of something you know, and something
you would like to ask.

00:31:34.000 --> 00:31:38.532
Keep those two things distinct: what you
already know from the student, and what

00:31:38.533 --> 00:31:42.491
you still need to explore. Sensory support
is about more than reducing

00:31:42.492 --> 00:31:46.679
difficulty. It can also make space for
pleasure and for ways of being that

00:31:46.680 --> 00:31:47.884
matter to the person.

00:31:49.400 --> 00:31:53.886
This slide introduces four sensory
response patterns from occupational

00:31:53.887 --> 00:31:58.502
therapist Winnie Dunn's framework. You may
encounter this language in an

00:31:58.503 --> 00:32:03.639
occupational therapy report or a Sensory
Profile School Companion questionnaire.

00:32:03.640 --> 00:32:08.711
The patterns are useful lenses, rather
than fixed types of person. Registration

00:32:08.712 --> 00:32:13.652
describes a pattern of noticing less
sensory information or missing cues that

00:32:13.653 --> 00:32:18.854
others notice. Seeking describes actively
looking for more input, perhaps through

00:32:18.855 --> 00:32:23.861
movement, touch or sound. Sensitivity
describes noticing sensory input readily

00:32:23.862 --> 00:32:28.543
and being affected by it, without
necessarily taking action to reduce it.

00:32:28.544 --> 00:32:33.614
Avoiding describes actively trying to
reduce input, perhaps by moving away from

00:32:33.615 --> 00:32:38.101
a crowd or covering the ears. The slide
also uses under-responsive and

00:32:38.102 --> 00:32:42.653
over-responsive language, and
distinguishes more passive responses from

00:32:42.654 --> 00:32:47.334
active ways of changing the input. Keep
that distinction connected to the

00:32:47.335 --> 00:32:51.886
student's experience, rather than treating
the labels as a judgement. A

00:32:51.887 --> 00:32:56.828
student may show different patterns for
different senses. Their responses may

00:32:56.829 --> 00:33:01.249
also change with the context, the load and
the day. We are looking at

00:33:01.250 --> 00:33:06.386
nervous-system responses, not simply a
decision to cooperate or avoid a task. So

00:33:06.387 --> 00:33:11.003
use the framework to sharpen your
questions. What input is present? What

00:33:11.004 --> 00:33:15.554
has the student communicated? What pattern
might be worth exploring? An

00:33:15.555 --> 00:33:20.431
observation gives us a starting point, not
certainty about what is happening

00:33:20.432 --> 00:33:20.886
inside.

00:33:22.400 --> 00:33:27.628
Let's apply that to a classroom routine.
Turn to the sensory activity on page

00:33:27.629 --> 00:33:32.995
seven of your Participant Guide, and work
in a small group. Choose one familiar

00:33:32.996 --> 00:33:38.155
routine that you can describe without
identifying a student or school. Start

00:33:38.156 --> 00:33:42.490
with the sensory demands of the
environment and the task, before

00:33:42.491 --> 00:33:47.788
discussing a student's response. What
might someone need to take in, filter or

00:33:47.789 --> 00:33:52.122
manage? You could consider sound,
lighting, movement, proximity,

00:33:52.123 --> 00:33:57.627
transitions or materials. These are areas
to investigate, not a universal list of

00:33:57.628 --> 00:34:02.993
triggers. Next, consider possible ways to
offer access or choice. How could the

00:34:02.994 --> 00:34:08.498
student communicate what works for them?
Treat each idea as a question to explore

00:34:08.499 --> 00:34:14.071
with the student, rather than a solution
you've already established. This is not a

00:34:14.072 --> 00:34:19.093
simulation of being Autistic. It is a
review of what the routine asks of a

00:34:19.094 --> 00:34:24.116
person. Choose the routine, map its
demands, then identify an option and a

00:34:24.117 --> 00:34:27.693
question you want to investigate. Begin
together now.

00:34:35.720 --> 00:34:40.836
Let's come back. Look at the option and
question your group has recorded. What

00:34:40.837 --> 00:34:45.621
could you change, offer or investigate
before increasing the demands on a

00:34:45.622 --> 00:34:50.406
student? Keep the sequence in mind:
examine the environment and the task,

00:34:50.407 --> 00:34:55.723
include the student's communication, and
review what actually helps. The point is

00:34:55.724 --> 00:35:01.040
to develop a more precise question about
access, with room for different students

00:35:01.041 --> 00:35:02.700
to need different things.

00:35:04.240 --> 00:35:08.860
We've been considering how the world
arrives through the senses. Now we'll

00:35:08.861 --> 00:35:13.164
turn to how the mind works with
information and where attention goes.

00:35:13.165 --> 00:35:18.164
Keep your students in mind as we explore
detail-first processing, deep focus and

00:35:18.165 --> 00:35:23.038
executive functioning. These concepts
won't describe every Autistic student in

00:35:23.039 --> 00:35:27.595
the same way. Use them to open inquiry,
and keep connecting them with the

00:35:27.596 --> 00:35:29.873
particular person, task and context.

00:35:31.400 --> 00:35:36.772
A detail-first approach offers one way of
understanding Autistic thinking. Top-down

00:35:36.773 --> 00:35:41.817
processing uses expectations and the wider
picture to help filter information.

00:35:41.818 --> 00:35:46.731
Bottom-up processing begins with the
details and builds towards the whole. A

00:35:46.732 --> 00:35:52.103
detail-first account places more emphasis
on that detailed information arriving and

00:35:52.104 --> 00:35:57.214
being worked through. That can help us
consider both depth and load. Noticing a

00:35:57.215 --> 00:36:02.586
change, an inconsistency or a pattern can
be a strength. Processing a great deal of

00:36:02.587 --> 00:36:07.173
detail can also make a situation
demanding. Neither conclusion tells us

00:36:07.174 --> 00:36:12.218
exactly what a particular student is
experiencing. The strengths on this slide

00:36:12.219 --> 00:36:17.263
include pattern recognition, systems
thinking, deep memory, logical precision,

00:36:17.264 --> 00:36:21.980
honesty and directness, depth of
connection, and intense engagement. Take

00:36:21.981 --> 00:36:27.222
each seriously as something valuable in
itself. Strengths are part of a student's

00:36:27.223 --> 00:36:32.070
present identity and learning, rather than
compensation for a difficulty or

00:36:32.071 --> 00:36:36.394
only future potential. You might recognise
connections with sensory

00:36:36.395 --> 00:36:40.981
intensity, literal language or deep focus.
Keep these as connections to

00:36:40.982 --> 00:36:46.026
explore, rather than assuming a thinking
process from behaviour alone. Turn to

00:36:46.027 --> 00:36:51.267
Strengths I can see on page eight of your
Participant Guide. For the students you

00:36:51.268 --> 00:36:56.116
have in mind, note the strengths you
recognise. What evidence supports that

00:36:56.117 --> 00:37:01.095
understanding? What might you be missing
if your attention is mainly drawn to

00:37:01.096 --> 00:37:01.816
difficulty?

00:37:09.840 --> 00:37:14.373
Keep those strengths visible as we
continue. They can help us recognise what

00:37:14.374 --> 00:37:18.363
a student brings to learning and
connection, while still taking the

00:37:18.364 --> 00:37:20.056
support they need seriously.

00:37:21.560 --> 00:37:26.116
Think for a moment about your own
attention. Does it move across many

00:37:26.117 --> 00:37:31.008
topics, or settle deeply into one? That
question introduces monotropism: a

00:37:31.009 --> 00:37:36.236
framework for understanding attention
focused deeply on fewer things at a time.

00:37:36.237 --> 00:37:41.463
Dinah Murray, Mike Lesser and Wenn Lawson
developed the framework, and Autistic

00:37:41.464 --> 00:37:46.556
community voices have continued to extend
it. One image is a torch beam and a

00:37:46.557 --> 00:37:52.051
floodlight. A floodlight spreads across a
wide area; a torch illuminates a narrower

00:37:52.052 --> 00:37:57.078
area intensely. Another is a river with
many channels compared with one deep

00:37:57.079 --> 00:38:01.970
channel. These are ways to picture
different patterns of attention, rather

00:38:01.971 --> 00:38:07.130
than a test for sorting people. Deep
interests can provide a strong route into

00:38:07.131 --> 00:38:12.492
learning, memory and joy. Flow, that full
absorption in an activity, is something

00:38:12.493 --> 00:38:17.786
worth protecting. It is more useful to
recognise that engagement than dismiss it

00:38:17.787 --> 00:38:23.013
as an obsession. Now picture a student
absorbed in a construction task, drawing

00:38:23.014 --> 00:38:28.173
or researching space. Asking them to stop
for recess may require a substantial

00:38:28.174 --> 00:38:32.798
shift. Tendril theory offers another
image: attention reaching into an

00:38:32.799 --> 00:38:38.092
activity, then needing time to withdraw
before moving elsewhere. Transitions can

00:38:38.093 --> 00:38:43.587
have a real cost. Warning and scaffolding
may help make that shift more manageable.

00:38:43.588 --> 00:38:48.345
Keep asking what support fits this student
and this moment. Detail-first

00:38:48.346 --> 00:38:53.171
processing helps us consider how
information arrives; deep focus helps us

00:38:53.172 --> 00:38:55.181
consider where attention goes.

00:38:56.720 --> 00:39:02.055
Executive functioning gives us another set
of questions about the demands of the

00:39:02.056 --> 00:39:07.323
school day. Let's name the ten areas on
the slide. Planning is working out what

00:39:07.324 --> 00:39:12.254
needs to happen. Organisation is arranging
materials and information. Time

00:39:12.255 --> 00:39:16.847
management involves judging and using
time. Working memory helps hold

00:39:16.848 --> 00:39:21.845
information in mind while we use it.
Metacognition means thinking about our

00:39:21.846 --> 00:39:26.708
own thinking. Emotional regulation
concerns managing emotional responses.

00:39:26.709 --> 00:39:31.774
Flexibility involves adapting when
something changes. Attention is directing

00:39:31.775 --> 00:39:36.569
and sustaining focus. Task initiation is
getting started. Persistence is

00:39:36.570 --> 00:39:41.972
continuing with a task. Now consider an
ordinary classroom activity. What does it

00:39:41.973 --> 00:39:47.308
ask a student to plan, start, remember,
sequence, organise, switch and complete?

00:39:47.309 --> 00:39:52.239
A seemingly simple instruction can contain
several demands. Interruptions,

00:39:52.240 --> 00:39:57.170
timing and the way instructions are
presented can change what is required.

00:39:57.171 --> 00:40:02.640
These terms are lenses for inquiry, not a
checklist to apply to a student. A delay

00:40:02.641 --> 00:40:08.044
in starting does not tell us, by itself,
that someone lacks effort or motivation.

00:40:08.045 --> 00:40:13.245
Useful support responds to the specific
demand in the specific context. As you

00:40:13.246 --> 00:40:18.648
look across the ten areas, consider where
each appears in the school day for your

00:40:18.649 --> 00:40:23.511
students, and where task design, timing or
support might make access more

00:40:23.512 --> 00:40:24.119
workable.

00:40:25.640 --> 00:40:29.964
Let's take getting started as one example.
The slide connects it with

00:40:29.965 --> 00:40:34.861
detail-first processing: starting can be
hard when the whole task is not clear

00:40:34.862 --> 00:40:39.504
yet. Some students may not be able to
identify the first step they need to

00:40:39.505 --> 00:40:43.702
take. That gives us a more useful question
than simply repeating an

00:40:43.703 --> 00:40:48.853
instruction to begin. Is the task clear?
Is its purpose and shape available to the

00:40:48.854 --> 00:40:53.623
student? Can they identify a first step?
Those questions don't establish the

00:40:53.624 --> 00:40:57.885
reason for a particular student's
difficulty. They give us something

00:40:57.886 --> 00:41:02.591
specific to check with them. Support can
then respond to the demand we have

00:41:02.592 --> 00:41:06.089
understood, rather than an assumption
about willingness.

00:41:07.600 --> 00:41:12.131
Switching focus gives us a second example.
It can be difficult for some

00:41:12.132 --> 00:41:17.116
students to stop what they are doing and
move their attention elsewhere. Think

00:41:17.117 --> 00:41:22.361
back to the student deeply absorbed in an
activity when recess begins. Transitions

00:41:22.362 --> 00:41:27.216
and unexpected interruptions can be
significant because attention is already

00:41:27.217 --> 00:41:32.396
deeply invested. The slide uses the image
of tunnel vision for the mind. It helps

00:41:32.397 --> 00:41:37.057
us consider the effort involved in
disengaging and redirecting attention.

00:41:37.058 --> 00:41:42.107
Keep the question practical: what is the
transition asking of this student, and

00:41:42.108 --> 00:41:47.157
what warning or scaffolding could make it
more manageable? A timetable tells us

00:41:47.158 --> 00:41:51.884
when the next activity starts. It doesn't,
by itself, tell us what someone

00:41:51.885 --> 00:41:54.085
needs in order to make that shift.

00:41:55.600 --> 00:42:00.305
A young person's account of deep focus and
transitions is planned for this point

00:42:00.306 --> 00:42:04.773
in the workshop, but the clip is not
included in the supplied materials yet.

00:42:04.774 --> 00:42:09.181
Hearing directly from Autistic people
remains central to this learning. The

00:42:09.182 --> 00:42:13.887
frameworks we've introduced can help us
form questions; they cannot stand in for

00:42:13.888 --> 00:42:18.176
a person's own account. Keep that
distinction as we move into discussion.

00:42:18.177 --> 00:42:22.525
What do you know from the students
themselves about their interests, their

00:42:22.526 --> 00:42:27.053
focus and the support they find useful?
Where do you still need to listen and

00:42:27.054 --> 00:42:27.410
learn?

00:42:28.920 --> 00:42:34.055
Turn to the thinking discussion on page
eight, and work with a partner. Hold your

00:42:34.056 --> 00:42:39.192
students in mind as you explore the four
questions on screen. What deep interests

00:42:39.193 --> 00:42:43.622
do they have? What do you notice when they
are fully engaged, and does

00:42:43.623 --> 00:42:48.566
engagement look the same for each of them?
When in the school day do they have

00:42:48.567 --> 00:42:53.188
trouble getting started or shifting
attention? And have you observed them

00:42:53.189 --> 00:42:58.132
fully absorbed in something at school?
What were they doing? Include strengths

00:42:58.133 --> 00:43:03.076
and flow alongside the parts that are
difficult. Separate what a task requires

00:43:03.077 --> 00:43:08.084
from what you observe a student doing, and
leave their internal experience open

00:43:08.085 --> 00:43:13.285
unless they have communicated it. If you
want to extend the discussion, choose one

00:43:13.286 --> 00:43:18.549
task and describe it neutrally. List what
it asks someone to notice, start, hold in

00:43:18.550 --> 00:43:23.493
mind, switch, sequence and complete. Mark
where the environment or instruction

00:43:23.494 --> 00:43:28.308
might reduce unnecessary demand, then
write one question to explore with the

00:43:28.309 --> 00:43:32.739
student or team. Begin with the four
questions, and leave time to note

00:43:32.740 --> 00:43:35.050
something you want to carry forward.

00:43:43.080 --> 00:43:47.691
Let's come back together. If you're
sharing, choose one brief observation or

00:43:47.692 --> 00:43:52.058
question from your discussion. Keep it
connected with a specific student

00:43:52.059 --> 00:43:56.916
experience or task, without identifying
the student. The purpose is to move from

00:43:56.917 --> 00:44:01.343
understanding a concept to recognising
something worth exploring in daily

00:44:01.344 --> 00:44:05.956
learning. Deep interests, starting,
shifting and flow can all help us notice

00:44:05.957 --> 00:44:10.568
more carefully. We can work with the
student's attention and strengths while

00:44:10.569 --> 00:44:15.488
considering the support they need. Keep
those notes for our next session, when we

00:44:15.489 --> 00:44:19.116
connect this thinking with communication
and the school day.

00:44:20.640 --> 00:44:25.253
The final part of this session turns
inward. We named interoception among the

00:44:25.254 --> 00:44:29.867
eight senses; now we'll return to it more
closely. What is happening inside a

00:44:29.868 --> 00:44:34.602
person may not be visible to an observer.
A student can be having an experience

00:44:34.603 --> 00:44:39.095
that we do not recognise from the surface.
Keep that in mind as we consider

00:44:39.096 --> 00:44:43.162
body signals, emotions and the ways we
build understanding together.

00:44:44.680 --> 00:44:49.631
Interoception is about understanding our
body's internal signals. It helps us

00:44:49.632 --> 00:44:54.975
answer the question, how am I doing? Am I
hot or cold? Am I hungry or thirsty? Do I

00:44:54.976 --> 00:44:59.601
need the bathroom? Am I tired? These
signals also contribute to noticing

00:44:59.602 --> 00:45:04.358
changes as we become upset and to
regulation. The other term on this slide

00:45:04.359 --> 00:45:09.245
is alexithymia: difficulty identifying and
describing emotions and feelings.

00:45:09.246 --> 00:45:14.263
Keep that definition clear. Identifying
what is happening and finding words to

00:45:14.264 --> 00:45:18.955
describe it can be difficult. The
questions on screen invite us to notice

00:45:18.956 --> 00:45:24.103
how much interpretation can be involved.
Am I excited or am I anxious? Is crying

00:45:24.104 --> 00:45:28.794
always sadness? What is my friend feeling?
We shouldn't assume the answer

00:45:28.795 --> 00:45:33.486
from outward behaviour. Crying alone, for
example, does not establish one

00:45:33.487 --> 00:45:38.439
particular feeling. And an apparently calm
surface doesn't tell us everything

00:45:38.440 --> 00:45:43.521
about what is happening inside. These
terms help us recognise why understanding

00:45:43.522 --> 00:45:48.409
internal experience matters. They are a
reason to stay open to the student's

00:45:48.410 --> 00:45:53.426
communication and to build understanding
together, rather than to decide their

00:45:53.427 --> 00:45:54.990
internal state for them.

00:45:56.520 --> 00:46:01.215
Sometimes a student experiences a build-up
inside, while the surface gives

00:46:01.216 --> 00:46:06.232
us little indication. If we wait until
distress is obvious, we may miss earlier

00:46:06.233 --> 00:46:11.121
opportunities to respond. The more we
understand a student's early signs, the

00:46:11.122 --> 00:46:15.430
better placed we are to recognise when
support might be useful. That

00:46:15.431 --> 00:46:20.705
understanding needs to be built with each
student, their family and other staff. No

00:46:20.706 --> 00:46:25.465
one person's observation gives us the
whole picture. Think of a shared map:

00:46:25.466 --> 00:46:30.096
what the student communicates, what
different people notice, what remains

00:46:30.097 --> 00:46:34.856
uncertain, and how people can respond
consistently. The map needs to remain

00:46:34.857 --> 00:46:39.359
open to revision as circumstances and
capacity change. An analogy or an

00:46:39.360 --> 00:46:43.475
observation can help start a conversation.
It doesn't replace the

00:46:43.476 --> 00:46:48.686
student's own communication. Keep asking
how the student can contribute, including

00:46:48.687 --> 00:46:53.832
in ways other than a spoken explanation,
and what the environment can do with the

00:46:53.833 --> 00:46:55.311
information they share.

00:46:56.840 --> 00:47:02.045
Turn to Reading the signs on page nine of
your Participant Guide, and work with a

00:47:02.046 --> 00:47:06.796
partner. For the students you have in
mind, what signs might you notice at

00:47:06.797 --> 00:47:11.416
school before they become overwhelmed? Are
those signs the same for each

00:47:11.417 --> 00:47:16.102
student? Use initials or roles in your
notes, and distinguish what you've

00:47:16.103 --> 00:47:21.113
observed from what you think it might
mean. Then consider what would be useful

00:47:21.114 --> 00:47:25.994
for other staff and families to know. What
have they noticed that could help

00:47:25.995 --> 00:47:31.200
you understand the picture? How could the
student communicate their capacity, and

00:47:31.201 --> 00:47:35.886
what could the environment do in response?
You don't need to settle on an

00:47:35.887 --> 00:47:40.571
explanation for every sign. Aim to
identify something useful to share and

00:47:40.572 --> 00:47:45.387
something you still need to explore
together. Take a few minutes with those

00:47:45.388 --> 00:47:46.298
questions now.

00:47:54.320 --> 00:47:59.069
Let's return. Keep one point that would be
useful to discuss with the student,

00:47:59.070 --> 00:48:03.635
their family or other staff. The signs may
differ between students, and the

00:48:03.636 --> 00:48:08.200
meaning of an observation needs to be
checked rather than assumed. A shared

00:48:08.201 --> 00:48:12.827
understanding can help people respond more
consistently and earlier. It also

00:48:12.828 --> 00:48:17.454
gives us a link to our later partnership
work: making room for the student's

00:48:17.455 --> 00:48:21.835
communication, bringing different
perspectives together, and considering

00:48:21.836 --> 00:48:23.994
what support the setting can offer.

00:48:25.520 --> 00:48:30.367
That brings us to morning tea. The
workshop schedule allows a break from ten

00:48:30.368 --> 00:48:35.215
forty-five to eleven; follow the timing
arranged for your session. After the

00:48:35.216 --> 00:48:39.869
break, we'll bring sensory experience,
thinking and internal signals into

00:48:39.870 --> 00:48:44.652
communication, connection and managing
daily capacity. We'll map demand and

00:48:44.653 --> 00:48:49.694
capacity across an ordinary school day,
keeping the same students in mind. Keep

00:48:49.695 --> 00:48:54.542
your notes. The strengths you've named,
the questions about routines and the

00:48:54.543 --> 00:48:59.002
things you still want to understand will
all be useful when we return.

00:49:07.040 --> 00:49:12.379
Welcome back. This is Autistic Experience
at School. You’ll find the Session Three

00:49:12.379 --> 00:49:17.190
summary on page ten of your Participant
Guide. Bring back the two or three

00:49:17.190 --> 00:49:21.870
students you’ve been holding in mind, as
different people with different

00:49:21.870 --> 00:49:26.682
strengths, preferences and experiences.
We’ll begin with communication and

00:49:26.682 --> 00:49:31.889
connection, then follow the school day as
students experience it. We’ll consider

00:49:31.889 --> 00:49:36.766
capacity, masking and belonging, widen our
attention to Autistic staff, and

00:49:36.766 --> 00:49:41.907
audit one school process. Your map and
audit will become the starting point for

00:49:41.907 --> 00:49:46.257
Learning Design after lunch. The
leadership question is what school

00:49:46.257 --> 00:49:51.069
conditions shape each person’s day. A bell
schedule tells us when a lesson

00:49:51.069 --> 00:49:55.419
starts. It cannot tell us everything about
what it takes to arrive,

00:49:55.419 --> 00:50:00.099
communicate and participate. One student’s
experience may prompt a wider

00:50:00.099 --> 00:50:05.174
question, while remaining that person’s
experience. Keep observations separate

00:50:05.174 --> 00:50:10.117
from assumptions, leave identifying
details out, and stay curious about what

00:50:10.117 --> 00:50:11.961
we still need to understand.

00:50:13.481 --> 00:50:18.564
Let’s begin with communication. Speech is
one way of communicating, and we’ll be

00:50:18.565 --> 00:50:23.325
looking at a much wider picture: how
students receive information, how they

00:50:23.326 --> 00:50:28.538
share what matters to them, and the ways
we make both possible. The amount someone

00:50:28.539 --> 00:50:33.428
speaks doesn’t tell us how much they
understand. A student may need different

00:50:33.429 --> 00:50:38.705
ways to understand an instruction, answer
a question, ask for help, request a pause

00:50:38.706 --> 00:50:43.724
or say no. All of those messages matter.
Communication access belongs in school

00:50:43.725 --> 00:50:48.293
design and school systems. We’ll talk
about practical options, including

00:50:48.294 --> 00:50:52.669
augmentative and alternative
communication, or A A C. There isn’t one

00:50:52.670 --> 00:50:57.302
approach that suits every student.
Individual assessment and intervention

00:50:57.303 --> 00:51:02.128
questions belong with the student’s speech
pathologist or relevant team; our

00:51:02.129 --> 00:51:06.375
focus here is what educators can notice,
make available and change.

00:51:07.881 --> 00:51:13.103
Let’s start with a quick, quiet think.
Since you woke up this morning, how have

00:51:13.104 --> 00:51:18.528
you communicated? See if you can think of
three different ways. Perhaps you spoke,

00:51:18.529 --> 00:51:23.617
sent a message, pointed, showed someone a
photo, moved away, or took a moment

00:51:23.618 --> 00:51:28.506
before answering. Give yourself about
twenty seconds. Then, if you’re with

00:51:28.507 --> 00:51:33.595
colleagues, two or three people might
share one example. Sharing is optional.

00:51:33.596 --> 00:51:37.880
Pause the video now if you’d like that
thinking and sharing time.

00:51:45.881 --> 00:51:51.576
Let’s come back together. We move between
ways of communicating all day, often

00:51:51.577 --> 00:51:56.088
without noticing. Writing, facial
expression, gesture, sign, a

00:51:56.089 --> 00:52:02.005
communication device and speech are all
real communication. One method isn’t more

00:52:02.006 --> 00:52:07.775
valid because it is spoken. That gives us
something useful to notice at school.

00:52:07.776 --> 00:52:13.101
When we say, ‘Use your words,’ are we
overlooking a message a student has

00:52:13.102 --> 00:52:18.796
already communicated? A point, a look, or
moving closer or further away can be

00:52:18.797 --> 00:52:24.788
worth attending to. Our job is to listen
across the ways that person communicates,

00:52:24.789 --> 00:52:28.929
while checking what a message means rather
than assuming.

00:52:30.440 --> 00:52:35.515
There are two parts to keep distinct here:
understanding and expressing.

00:52:35.516 --> 00:52:41.092
Understanding is information coming in and
making sense. That might be a spoken

00:52:41.093 --> 00:52:46.311
instruction, a written sequence, a
demonstration, or a gesture in context.

00:52:46.312 --> 00:52:51.817
Expressing is a message going out: an
idea, a feeling, a question or a choice,

00:52:51.818 --> 00:52:57.393
through any communication method. These
don’t necessarily look the same for one

00:52:57.394 --> 00:53:02.970
person. Someone may speak at length and
still need support to understand spoken

00:53:02.971 --> 00:53:08.690
information. Someone else may understand
much more than they can readily express.

00:53:08.691 --> 00:53:14.481
Neither fluent speech nor limited speech
gives us the whole picture. Communication

00:53:14.482 --> 00:53:19.701
can also change with the person, the
place, the demands and how someone is

00:53:19.702 --> 00:53:25.277
feeling. A A C means augmentative and
alternative communication. It can include

00:53:25.278 --> 00:53:30.997
gesture, signs, a communication board or
book, typing, or a communication device.

00:53:30.998 --> 00:53:36.288
Some people use it alongside speech and
some instead of speech. Repeated or

00:53:36.289 --> 00:53:41.436
scripted words can carry meaning too.
Listen for the context and possible

00:53:41.437 --> 00:53:45.726
purpose before correcting the form. We
don’t need to assign a

00:53:45.727 --> 00:53:50.873
language-processing label before
respecting a student’s communication. If

00:53:50.874 --> 00:53:56.379
we need an individual assessment, we
involve the relevant team. For our school

00:53:56.380 --> 00:54:02.170
practice, the question is: what does this
environment help the student understand,

00:54:02.171 --> 00:54:07.461
and what ways does it give them to
respond? Look at routines, documents and

00:54:07.462 --> 00:54:12.537
expectations too. Where do they assume
that speaking is the usual way to

00:54:12.538 --> 00:54:13.395
participate?

00:54:14.920 --> 00:54:19.160
We’re going to hear Autistic people
describe communication in their own

00:54:19.160 --> 00:54:23.764
words. As you watch, notice what supports
communication. Choose one idea that

00:54:23.764 --> 00:54:28.307
stands out to you. Let the speakers’
experiences lead your thinking, without

00:54:28.307 --> 00:54:31.638
expecting one account to describe every
Autistic person.

01:00:04.120 --> 01:00:08.620
What stood out to you about what supported
communication? Take a moment to

01:00:08.621 --> 01:00:13.614
choose one idea. You might think about a
communication strength, something another

01:00:13.615 --> 01:00:17.868
person did that helped, or the difference
the environment or available

01:00:17.869 --> 01:00:22.492
communication method made. If you’re
watching with colleagues, invite one or

01:00:22.493 --> 01:00:25.760
two brief responses. Pause here for that
conversation.

01:00:33.760 --> 01:00:38.526
As we come back, hold onto that specific
idea. There is no single Autistic

01:00:38.527 --> 01:00:43.161
communication profile, so we need to
listen to what helps the particular

01:00:43.162 --> 01:00:48.124
person. One practical point to carry into
school is availability. A student’s

01:00:48.125 --> 01:00:52.890
communication system needs to be where
communication happens: in class, at

01:00:52.891 --> 01:00:57.788
break, during transitions and when plans
change. It cannot be limited to one

01:00:57.789 --> 01:01:02.815
activity or a therapy session. We support
the student’s ways of communicating,

01:01:02.816 --> 01:01:07.386
working with the student, their family and
the relevant team. We aren’t

01:01:07.387 --> 01:01:12.348
prescribing a system from a workshop
example. As leaders, ask how that access

01:01:12.349 --> 01:01:16.984
continues across staff, places and
routines. Who needs to understand the

01:01:16.985 --> 01:01:21.359
student’s communication, and how will
useful information reach them?

01:01:22.881 --> 01:01:28.095
Turn to page eleven of your Participant
Guide, or use a sheet of paper. Put one

01:01:28.096 --> 01:01:33.176
of the students you have in mind in the
centre. Around them are home, school,

01:01:33.177 --> 01:01:38.191
friends, community and supports. Who is
part of this student’s communication

01:01:38.192 --> 01:01:43.004
network? Add just one person or place now.
Use roles rather than names: a

01:01:43.005 --> 01:01:48.286
family member, a classmate, a teacher,
someone at a club. You can write, draw or

01:01:48.287 --> 01:01:53.301
use dot points. If it helps, ask a
colleague to scribe your ideas. You don’t

01:01:53.302 --> 01:01:58.583
need to fill every space. Networks can be
small or changing, and an empty box is

01:01:58.584 --> 01:02:03.998
fine. Give yourself about a minute and a
half. Pause the video while you make that

01:02:03.999 --> 01:02:05.000
first addition.

01:02:13.001 --> 01:02:17.823
Keep that map open. You can return to the
rest later. Different people and places

01:02:17.824 --> 01:02:22.284
may reveal different parts of a student’s
communication. A family member, a

01:02:22.285 --> 01:02:27.046
classroom teacher and a support worker may
each notice something valuable. We’re

01:02:27.047 --> 01:02:31.869
not deciding who understands the student
best. We’re recognising that the picture

01:02:31.870 --> 01:02:36.510
is shared. Where we know little about part
of that network, we have a question

01:02:36.511 --> 01:02:40.851
to explore with the student and family,
rather than a gap to fill with an

01:02:40.852 --> 01:02:41.513
assumption.

01:02:43.041 --> 01:02:48.037
Now let’s think about what should travel
between those people and places, so the

01:02:48.038 --> 01:02:52.654
student doesn’t have to start again with
every new person. We’ll use three

01:02:52.655 --> 01:02:57.209
colours. Purple is a communication
strength. Blue is something that helps

01:02:57.210 --> 01:03:01.889
communication. Orange is something you
would like others to know. Write one

01:03:01.890 --> 01:03:06.760
note, two, or all three. You can use one
of your students, without identifying

01:03:06.761 --> 01:03:11.567
them, or a made-up example. If you don’t
have coloured notes, label the three

01:03:11.568 --> 01:03:16.438
categories on paper. This is also on page
eleven. Allow about three minutes to

01:03:16.439 --> 01:03:21.435
write. Then, if you’re together, spend a
few minutes sharing. You can add a note

01:03:21.436 --> 01:03:26.495
to the board or read one out, and you can
keep your notes private. Leave any note

01:03:26.496 --> 01:03:31.239
with identifying details off the shared
board. Pause now for the writing and

01:03:31.240 --> 01:03:32.311
optional sharing.

01:03:40.320 --> 01:03:45.052
Let’s bring our attention back. Look
across the ideas available to you. What

01:03:45.053 --> 01:03:49.469
similarities do you notice? What strengths
might be missed if we looked

01:03:49.470 --> 01:03:54.518
only for speech? Which supports involve
changing what other people do or provide?

01:03:54.519 --> 01:03:58.809
Think about what would help a relief
teacher, a specialist teacher or

01:03:58.810 --> 01:04:03.794
playground staff. Useful knowledge can
travel between classrooms, the playground

01:04:03.795 --> 01:04:08.527
and home, with the student’s and family’s
perspectives included. It can make

01:04:08.528 --> 01:04:13.134
continuity possible when the people around
a student change. Keep the blue

01:04:13.135 --> 01:04:17.677
notes in view: the things that help
communication. We’re going to connect

01:04:17.678 --> 01:04:22.600
those ideas with three practical options.
Now add the leadership question. What

01:04:22.601 --> 01:04:27.396
system carries this information in your
school, and where does it rely on one

01:04:27.397 --> 01:04:32.318
person remembering? Student profiles,
handover notes and relief-day information

01:04:32.319 --> 01:04:37.177
can support continuity. Check how the
student’s and family’s knowledge reaches

01:04:37.178 --> 01:04:41.341
the people who need it through your
school’s established processes.

01:04:42.880 --> 01:04:48.525
The first option is clear communication.
Use familiar, specific words and give one

01:04:48.526 --> 01:04:53.892
idea or step at a time, in a form and
amount the person can access. That might

01:04:53.893 --> 01:04:59.328
mean making a direction shorter or making
the first step clearer. The second is

01:04:59.329 --> 01:05:03.789
processing time. After giving information,
pause. A person may be

01:05:03.790 --> 01:05:09.295
making sense of what you said, organising
a response and communicating it. If we

01:05:09.296 --> 01:05:14.802
fill every pause with a new version of the
question, we may add more information

01:05:14.803 --> 01:05:20.238
before the earlier information has been
processed. The third is visual support:

01:05:20.239 --> 01:05:25.953
showing information as well as saying it,
so there is something to return to. These

01:05:25.954 --> 01:05:31.390
are options to match to the person and the
moment, rather than a checklist that

01:05:31.391 --> 01:05:36.966
guarantees success. Keep the student’s A A
C available and accept their preferred

01:05:36.967 --> 01:05:42.124
ways of responding, including refusal. A
useful communication support makes

01:05:42.125 --> 01:05:47.770
messages easier to understand and share.
It doesn’t require the student to produce

01:05:47.771 --> 01:05:52.997
the answer or communication style we were
expecting. When these supports are

01:05:52.998 --> 01:05:58.294
expected across the school, a student’s
access is less dependent on one staff

01:05:58.295 --> 01:06:03.173
member. Leaders can ask where that
consistency already exists and where

01:06:03.174 --> 01:06:05.542
staff need information or support.

01:06:07.080 --> 01:06:12.243
Visual supports are information you can
see and come back to. The three examples

01:06:12.244 --> 01:06:17.015
here answer different questions. A timer
can show how much time is left. A

01:06:17.016 --> 01:06:22.048
sequence can show what to do next. A daily
schedule can show what is happening

01:06:22.049 --> 01:06:27.343
today. You may already use a calendar, a
shopping list or a reminder on your phone

01:06:27.344 --> 01:06:32.245
in much the same way. The format needs to
make sense to the person. Objects,

01:06:32.246 --> 01:06:37.083
photographs, written words and symbols can
all be options, on paper or on a

01:06:37.084 --> 01:06:41.135
device. Check that the student understands
what the information

01:06:41.136 --> 01:06:46.103
represents and can reach it when it is
needed. A support displayed across the

01:06:46.104 --> 01:06:51.137
room may exist without being useful at the
moment of need. Think of the visual

01:06:51.138 --> 01:06:56.301
information already available across your
school, including timetables, signage,

01:06:56.302 --> 01:07:01.072
assemblies and notices home. What question
does it answer? Could a student

01:07:01.073 --> 01:07:06.040
return to it without asking an adult? A
visual support offers information; it

01:07:06.041 --> 01:07:10.812
isn’t a condition the student must satisfy
to access something they enjoy.

01:07:10.813 --> 01:07:15.976
Visual supports and A A C can overlap, but
they aren’t interchangeable. Keep the

01:07:15.977 --> 01:07:21.205
person’s communication methods available
as well. We’ll return to this in Session

01:07:21.206 --> 01:07:24.866
Four when you choose a design option for a
school system.

01:07:26.400 --> 01:07:31.018
Double empathy asks us to look at
understanding in both directions. When

01:07:31.019 --> 01:07:35.962
people have different experiences and ways
of communicating, it can be harder

01:07:35.963 --> 01:07:40.191
for them to understand one another. That
doesn’t mean every social

01:07:40.192 --> 01:07:45.526
misunderstanding sits inside the Autistic
student. Consider a classroom discussion,

01:07:45.527 --> 01:07:50.210
group task or playground interaction.
Whose expectations are shaping what

01:07:50.211 --> 01:07:55.415
counts as joining in? Who is expected to
adjust? What would make this interaction

01:07:55.416 --> 01:08:00.424
clearer or safer for the people involved?
For example, an adult might expect a

01:08:00.425 --> 01:08:04.913
quick spoken response as a sign of
connection. A student might connect

01:08:04.914 --> 01:08:09.727
through a shared activity, through
detailed talk about an interest, or with

01:08:09.728 --> 01:08:14.281
more time between turns. Those are
possible differences to explore, not

01:08:14.282 --> 01:08:19.226
conclusions to impose on a particular
child. The question is how each student

01:08:19.227 --> 01:08:24.561
connects. Connection doesn’t need to look
one way. Our expectations and the context

01:08:24.562 --> 01:08:29.505
shape the interaction too, so we have
something useful to examine and change.

01:08:29.506 --> 01:08:34.645
We can make room for different ways of
being together while continuing to listen

01:08:34.646 --> 01:08:39.979
to what each person actually wants. Which
school practices favour one social style?

01:08:39.980 --> 01:08:44.859
Think about how groups are formed, what is
recognised at assemblies and what

01:08:44.860 --> 01:08:49.283
counts as participation. These are
expectations a leadership team can

01:08:49.284 --> 01:08:52.080
examine with the people who experience
them.

01:08:53.601 --> 01:08:59.165
Let’s carry that idea into the many ways
students connect. As we watch this next

01:08:59.166 --> 01:09:04.379
clip, keep the question open: how does
each student connect? Notice people,

01:09:04.380 --> 01:09:09.803
places, activities and ways of joining. A
different style of connection is not

01:09:09.804 --> 01:09:12.268
evidence that connection is absent.

01:09:57.361 --> 01:10:01.576
Keep those possibilities in mind as we
widen the picture beyond a single

01:10:01.577 --> 01:10:06.207
interaction. We can look for connection in
a student’s preferred activities and

01:10:06.208 --> 01:10:10.362
relationships, while allowing their own
ways of showing it to guide us.

01:10:11.881 --> 01:10:16.163
A student’s social world can include
family, school, community and online

01:10:16.164 --> 01:10:20.684
spaces. Connection might be one to one,
playing or working alongside someone,

01:10:20.685 --> 01:10:25.146
sharing a great deal about an interest, or
seeing someone only occasionally.

01:10:25.147 --> 01:10:29.846
Being together quietly can matter as much
as a lively conversation. Culture also

01:10:29.847 --> 01:10:34.426
shapes expectations about interaction and
relationships. We need to be careful

01:10:34.427 --> 01:10:38.828
about treating the pattern most familiar
to us as the measure of meaningful

01:10:38.829 --> 01:10:43.290
connection for everyone. There may be
spaces outside home and school where a

01:10:43.291 --> 01:10:47.752
student feels a particular sense of
belonging: a club, a community activity,

01:10:47.753 --> 01:10:52.392
or another shared-interest space. There
may be online connections too. We don’t

01:10:52.393 --> 01:10:56.318
need to disclose a student’s online
activity, identity or community

01:10:56.319 --> 01:11:00.542
membership to recognise that these
possibilities exist. Keep the picture

01:11:00.543 --> 01:11:05.063
individual. What do we know about this
student’s preferred people, places and

01:11:05.064 --> 01:11:09.465
ways of joining? What are we assuming?
What could we find out respectfully?

01:11:11.001 --> 01:11:15.936
Find a partner for a conversation about
connection. The questions are on page

01:11:15.937 --> 01:11:20.938
twelve of your Participant Guide. First,
when do the students you have in mind

01:11:20.939 --> 01:11:25.874
seem most comfortable connecting with
others at school? Second, what kinds of

01:11:25.875 --> 01:11:31.006
connection does each enjoy? That might be
talking, gaming, sport, making things,

01:11:31.007 --> 01:11:35.617
shared interests, or being together
quietly. Third, are there times when

01:11:35.618 --> 01:11:40.554
being social seems enjoyable, and times
when it seems tiring or overwhelming?

01:11:40.555 --> 01:11:45.490
Finally, what could educators change to
make connection feel safer, easier or

01:11:45.491 --> 01:11:50.491
more meaningful? Use about five minutes.
Keep identifying details private, and

01:11:50.492 --> 01:11:55.298
hold anything you don’t know as a
question. You can reflect individually if

01:11:55.299 --> 01:11:59.390
you’re watching on your own. Pause the
video for the discussion.

01:12:07.400 --> 01:12:12.323
Let’s come back to the last question: what
could educators change around the

01:12:12.324 --> 01:12:17.378
student? If you’re together, you might
offer one practical possibility without

01:12:17.379 --> 01:12:22.237
describing the student. Notice whether
your idea makes room for the kind of

01:12:22.238 --> 01:12:27.161
connection the student values. Does it
change an expectation, an activity, a

01:12:27.162 --> 01:12:32.479
space or the pace of interaction? Does it
leave room for choosing when to join and

01:12:32.480 --> 01:12:37.797
when to step back? The aim is meaningful
connection for that person. We don’t need

01:12:37.798 --> 01:12:42.852
to make everyone look social in the same
way. At school level, that could mean

01:12:42.853 --> 01:12:48.038
examining lunchtime clubs, quiet spaces at
break or how groups are formed. Treat

01:12:48.039 --> 01:12:53.159
each as a possibility to discuss with
students, rather than deciding in advance

01:12:53.160 --> 01:12:55.325
what connection should look like.

01:12:56.841 --> 01:13:02.478
Now we’ll look at daily capacity.
Communication and connection are part of

01:13:02.479 --> 01:13:07.730
a much larger day, and that day asks
different amounts of energy from

01:13:07.731 --> 01:13:13.446
different students. As we move into the
next activities, we’ll use a simple

01:13:13.447 --> 01:13:19.625
question: what costs, and what lightens?
That language will help us look beyond a

01:13:19.626 --> 01:13:25.649
single task to the pattern of demands and
opportunities for recovery across the

01:13:25.650 --> 01:13:25.957
day.

01:13:27.481 --> 01:13:32.277
Think of a student’s available energy as a
battery. Each day starts with some

01:13:32.278 --> 01:13:37.074
charge, and it isn’t necessarily the same
amount as yesterday. Activities and

01:13:37.075 --> 01:13:41.618
demands draw on that energy. As the
battery runs lower, managing the next

01:13:41.619 --> 01:13:46.352
demand can become harder. You may also
hear people in the Autistic community

01:13:46.353 --> 01:13:51.338
talk about spoons. The idea is having a
limited supply available: an action uses

01:13:51.339 --> 01:13:56.199
one spoon or several, and when spoons are
low, there is less left for the next

01:13:56.200 --> 01:14:00.995
thing. These are ways of talking about
capacity, rather than a measure we can

01:14:00.996 --> 01:14:05.729
calculate for someone else. The other part
of the picture is recharging. The

01:14:05.730 --> 01:14:10.716
right supports can reduce demand and help
restore some capacity. A pause, useful

01:14:10.717 --> 01:14:15.260
information, a preferred way to
communicate or a safe connection may make

01:14:15.261 --> 01:14:20.183
a difference, depending on the person. So
we’ll look for both sides in a school

01:14:20.184 --> 01:14:24.728
day: what draws on capacity, and what
helps to restore it. What helps one

01:14:24.729 --> 01:14:27.061
student may be different for another.

01:14:28.601 --> 01:14:33.589
Capacity fluctuates. Like all of us,
Autistic students can have easier days

01:14:33.590 --> 01:14:38.781
and harder days. Stress, energy, sensory
demands and what else is happening in

01:14:38.782 --> 01:14:44.107
life can change what is manageable. That
means the same student may do something

01:14:44.108 --> 01:14:49.501
one day and need more support with it the
next. Yesterday’s success doesn’t prove

01:14:49.502 --> 01:14:54.963
that today’s difficulty is a choice, and
finishing a task doesn’t tell us how much

01:14:54.964 --> 01:15:00.087
effort it required. Hold that in mind when
you look at the students you know.

01:15:00.088 --> 01:15:05.278
We can ask what is different today, what
demands have already accumulated, and

01:15:05.279 --> 01:15:10.470
what support is available now. We don’t
have to know every cause before making

01:15:10.471 --> 01:15:15.797
information clearer or allowing a useful
pause. We do need to keep checking with

01:15:15.798 --> 01:15:21.122
the student and the people who know them,
instead of treating capacity as fixed.

01:15:22.640 --> 01:15:28.096
Turn to page thirteen. Follow a typical
school day as your students experience

01:15:28.097 --> 01:15:33.695
it, from arrival to departure. Include
whole-school routines: bells, assemblies,

01:15:33.696 --> 01:15:38.939
breaks, relief days and changes. A primary
setting might include specialist

01:15:38.940 --> 01:15:44.255
lessons and the playground. A secondary
setting might involve moving between

01:15:44.256 --> 01:15:49.499
rooms and teachers each period. Mark R for
a routine or transition, C for a

01:15:49.500 --> 01:15:53.964
change, T for a communication opportunity,
and P for a moment to

01:15:53.965 --> 01:15:59.492
choose, pause or recover. Then consider
how the same moments affect each of the

01:15:59.493 --> 01:16:04.949
students you have in mind. Follow their
experience, rather than simply copying

01:16:04.950 --> 01:16:10.194
the official timetable. Keep unknowns as
questions. Now step back. Where do

01:16:10.195 --> 01:16:15.721
demands or requests for support seem to
cluster across the school: a particular

01:16:15.722 --> 01:16:21.320
period, day of the week, or point in the
term? Draw on patterns you already know

01:16:21.321 --> 01:16:25.856
in general. This is a prompt for examining
school design, not for

01:16:25.857 --> 01:16:31.171
monitoring or labelling individual
students. Allow about seven minutes. Keep

01:16:31.172 --> 01:16:36.841
identifying details out, leave room for
the next layer, and pause the video while

01:16:36.842 --> 01:16:37.408
you map.

01:16:45.440 --> 01:16:50.367
Let’s pause our drawing there. You don’t
need a polished or complete map. This is

01:16:50.368 --> 01:16:55.110
a working picture to extend, rather than a
judgement of a student or a school.

01:16:55.111 --> 01:16:59.791
Look briefly at the same point in the day
for your different students. It may

01:16:59.792 --> 01:17:04.780
involve different communication, sensory
or task demands for each of them. That is

01:17:04.781 --> 01:17:09.708
why we’re keeping several real students in
view. Keep the map beside you; it will

01:17:09.709 --> 01:17:14.635
travel with us into Session Four. Look at
any school-wide pattern you noted. What

01:17:14.636 --> 01:17:18.762
question does it raise about the bell
schedule, assemblies or relief

01:17:18.763 --> 01:17:23.136
arrangements? The pattern gives us
somewhere to investigate. It does not

01:17:23.137 --> 01:17:26.585
establish the cause of a particular
student’s experience.

01:17:28.121 --> 01:17:33.149
Now turn to page fourteen and return to
your day map. Use one mark or colour for

01:17:33.150 --> 01:17:37.223
what costs: what adds demand or
uncertainty. Use another for what

01:17:37.224 --> 01:17:42.316
lightens: what reduces demand, restores
capacity or brings joy. Think back to the

01:17:42.317 --> 01:17:46.963
battery image: what drains, and what might
recharge? Add questions where a

01:17:46.964 --> 01:17:50.910
student’s experience isn’t known.
Something can be enjoyable or

01:17:50.911 --> 01:17:55.748
successfully completed and still take
considerable effort. We can record what

01:17:55.749 --> 01:18:00.587
we observe, but we can’t read an internal
state from a single moment. Include

01:18:00.588 --> 01:18:05.107
strengths, joy, safe connection and
belonging. We want a picture of what

01:18:05.108 --> 01:18:09.690
sustains a student as well as what is
difficult. Give yourself about five

01:18:09.691 --> 01:18:13.445
minutes to add this layer, and pause the
video while you do.

01:18:21.480 --> 01:18:26.249
Come back to the pattern across the day.
Where do several demands occur close

01:18:26.250 --> 01:18:31.018
together? Where are the opportunities for
relief or recovery? What is already

01:18:31.019 --> 01:18:36.102
working well and worth protecting? Treat
these marks as prompts for inquiry, not a

01:18:36.103 --> 01:18:40.746
score. What costs or lightens can differ
between students, and for the same

01:18:40.747 --> 01:18:45.327
student on different days. Choose one
pattern you would like to understand

01:18:45.328 --> 01:18:50.348
better. That will help when we examine a
school process shortly. Ask where access

01:18:50.349 --> 01:18:54.992
depends on one person, and where the
school provides recovery opportunities

01:18:54.993 --> 01:18:59.824
students can use more dependably. What is
already built into the day, and what

01:18:59.825 --> 01:19:02.021
depends on a student having to ask?

01:19:03.560 --> 01:19:08.925
This school-day stack gives us another way
to see accumulation. The darker bars

01:19:08.926 --> 01:19:14.016
represent demand. The lighter bars
represent relief and recovery. They’re a

01:19:14.017 --> 01:19:19.451
visual cue, rather than quantities we can
assign to a child. A moment that looks

01:19:19.452 --> 01:19:24.817
small by itself may arrive after many
other demands. The last transition of the

01:19:24.818 --> 01:19:29.495
day sits within everything that came
before it. Equally, a supportive

01:19:29.496 --> 01:19:34.861
interaction or an opportunity to recover
belongs in the picture too. So when we

01:19:34.862 --> 01:19:40.021
notice a difficult moment, we can ask what
the day has already asked of this

01:19:40.022 --> 01:19:44.974
student and what support has been
available. Keep this qualitative. We’re

01:19:44.975 --> 01:19:50.203
looking for patterns and questions that
help us improve access, while keeping

01:19:50.204 --> 01:19:54.675
relief, recovery and joy visible alongside
cost. From a leadership

01:19:54.676 --> 01:19:59.903
position, consider what bell times,
assemblies and relief arrangements add to

01:19:59.904 --> 01:20:05.063
this pattern. Where does the school day or
week offer relief? A whole-school

01:20:05.064 --> 01:20:10.153
routine can affect many students at once,
while affecting each differently.

01:20:11.681 --> 01:20:16.170
There are other things that can draw on
our batteries or spoons, including effort

01:20:16.171 --> 01:20:20.604
that isn’t obvious to someone watching.
Let’s hear the next video about masking.

01:20:20.605 --> 01:20:25.037
Keep in mind the difference between how a
person appears on the outside and what

01:20:25.038 --> 01:20:26.215
they may be managing.

01:21:56.641 --> 01:22:02.228
As we return from the video, keep that
distinction open. Visible behaviour gives

01:22:02.229 --> 01:22:07.392
us part of the picture. We’ll now connect
this with identity, capacity and

01:22:07.393 --> 01:22:08.807
belonging at school.

01:22:10.320 --> 01:22:15.992
A student can appear fine without feeling
fine. Quietness, compliance or completed

01:22:15.993 --> 01:22:21.454
work doesn’t give us a full account of
what someone is managing. Masking brings

01:22:21.455 --> 01:22:26.357
that question into our thinking about
identity and belonging. Hold four

01:22:26.358 --> 01:22:31.259
questions in mind. What might this student
be working hard to manage or

01:22:31.260 --> 01:22:36.791
hide? Where can they be recognised and
included? What supports belonging without

01:22:36.792 --> 01:22:42.324
requiring them to appear less Autistic?
And what does the student want others to

01:22:42.325 --> 01:22:47.226
understand? We can’t answer those
questions simply by watching from the

01:22:47.227 --> 01:22:52.339
outside. They invite us to listen and to
examine the conditions around the

01:22:52.340 --> 01:22:57.591
student. Include what feels safe and
joyful, the connections they value, and

01:22:57.592 --> 01:23:03.334
places where they can be themselves. This
is a reason to look beyond whether a task

01:23:03.335 --> 01:23:08.306
was finished or a lesson was quiet. Access
matters, and so do the effort

01:23:08.307 --> 01:23:13.349
involved and the student’s experience of
belonging. Leaders cannot know a

01:23:13.350 --> 01:23:18.741
student’s experience by watching from an
office, but they can shape conditions

01:23:18.742 --> 01:23:24.344
where different ways of participating are
accepted. Ask what the school can do to

01:23:24.345 --> 01:23:29.736
reduce the need to hide difficulty. If
this reflection feels personal, you are

01:23:29.737 --> 01:23:33.168
welcome to step out and return when you
are ready.

01:23:34.681 --> 01:23:39.654
Return to page twelve for the masking
discussion. With a partner, consider

01:23:39.655 --> 01:23:45.037
three questions. When might one of the
students you have in mind be working hard

01:23:45.038 --> 01:23:50.147
to appear fine at school when they are
not? When and where do they seem most

01:23:50.148 --> 01:23:55.052
comfortable being themselves? What could
we change to help them feel more

01:23:55.053 --> 01:24:00.435
comfortable and supported? You have about
four minutes. Keep examples anonymised

01:24:00.436 --> 01:24:05.750
and distinguish what you know from what
you wonder. Avoid assuming that a quiet

01:24:05.751 --> 01:24:10.519
student is coping, or that outward
behaviour tells you the whole story.

01:24:10.520 --> 01:24:13.039
Pause the video for the conversation.

01:24:21.041 --> 01:24:26.638
Let’s come back together. Focus on one
possible change, rather than a conclusion

01:24:26.639 --> 01:24:32.378
about what a student must be feeling. If
you’re sharing with colleagues, offer the

01:24:32.379 --> 01:24:37.905
change without identifying the child.
Perhaps your next step is to ask a better

01:24:37.906 --> 01:24:43.149
question, understand an existing source of
comfort, or change a demand in a

01:24:43.150 --> 01:24:48.747
routine. Whatever you choose, keep the
student’s perspective central. The aim is

01:24:48.748 --> 01:24:54.345
to make being themselves safer and better
supported, and to find out whether the

01:24:54.346 --> 01:24:55.266
change helps.

01:24:56.800 --> 01:25:01.612
Let’s widen the picture to Autistic staff.
The thinking we have used about

01:25:01.613 --> 01:25:06.689
sensory load, masking, communication and
energy also matters in staffrooms and

01:25:06.690 --> 01:25:11.437
meetings. Your school may include Autistic
staff who have disclosed that,

01:25:11.438 --> 01:25:16.580
and staff who have not. Their access and
flourishing matter in their own right.

01:25:16.581 --> 01:25:21.789
The strengths on the slide include deep
knowledge, precision and connection with

01:25:21.790 --> 01:25:26.074
neurodivergent students. These are
possibilities to recognise, not

01:25:26.075 --> 01:25:30.888
assumptions to assign to a person.
Strengths and support needs can both be

01:25:30.889 --> 01:25:36.163
present. Consider predictable meetings,
clear communication, choices about how to

01:25:36.164 --> 01:25:41.240
contribute and access to quiet space. Many
of these practices can be available

01:25:41.241 --> 01:25:45.591
without anyone needing to disclose
personal information. Individual

01:25:45.592 --> 01:25:50.668
workplace adjustments also follow the
processes in your school or system. Take

01:25:50.669 --> 01:25:55.350
about two minutes privately. Choose an
ordinary staff process, such as a

01:25:55.351 --> 01:25:59.899
meeting, the way changes are communicated,
or how relief is allocated.

01:25:59.900 --> 01:26:05.108
What is already clear and predictable?
Where is flexibility possible? What would

01:26:05.109 --> 01:26:09.922
someone have to ask for, or disclose, to
get what they need? Who should be

01:26:09.923 --> 01:26:14.999
involved before changing that process?
Keep this reflection about the process.

01:26:15.000 --> 01:26:20.142
Please do not identify or speculate about
colleagues. Pause the video while you

01:26:20.143 --> 01:26:20.669
reflect.

01:26:28.680 --> 01:26:33.295
Keep that reflection with you. You can
carry a question about the process into

01:26:33.296 --> 01:26:38.031
the design work without sharing anyone’s
personal circumstances. A culture where

01:26:38.032 --> 01:26:42.047
people can be open begins with what
happens in everyday routines and

01:26:42.048 --> 01:26:46.363
conversations. Build that safety, and
never ask anyone to disclose. We’ll

01:26:46.364 --> 01:26:50.320
return to the part leaders play in shaping
culture in Session Four.

01:26:51.840 --> 01:26:57.311
Turn to page fifteen for Audit at school
level. Choose one process: enrolment and

01:26:57.312 --> 01:27:02.303
starting at the school, assemblies,
relief-day arrangements, how change is

01:27:02.304 --> 01:27:07.570
communicated, or where students can go to
regulate. Work on your own or with a

01:27:07.571 --> 01:27:12.768
colleague. First, name the process. Follow
what a student encounters from the

01:27:12.769 --> 01:27:17.966
first point of contact through to the next
handover or outcome. Second, write

01:27:17.967 --> 01:27:22.754
what educators can observe without
interpretation. Third, list possible

01:27:22.755 --> 01:27:27.883
contributors in the environment or task,
including timing, communication and

01:27:27.884 --> 01:27:32.534
expectations. Fourth, record what lightens
demand and one question to

01:27:32.535 --> 01:27:37.937
investigate. Where is access built into
the process? Where does it depend on one

01:27:37.938 --> 01:27:42.793
person remembering, or on a student
asking? For example, in a relief-day

01:27:42.794 --> 01:27:48.265
process, you might ask who tells students
about the change and whether the relief

01:27:48.266 --> 01:27:53.326
teacher receives useful information. In a
regulation-space process, ask how

01:27:53.327 --> 01:27:58.114
students reach it and when it is
available. Those are examples to guide

01:27:58.115 --> 01:28:03.449
your questions, not findings about your
school. Allow about seven minutes. Keep

01:28:03.450 --> 01:28:08.715
the audit reflective and anonymised. We
are examining the design of a process,

01:28:08.716 --> 01:28:14.118
not ranking the school or evaluating a
named staff member. Pause the video while

01:28:14.119 --> 01:28:14.734
you work.

01:28:22.760 --> 01:28:28.230
Let’s bring that audit to a close. What
did it confirm, and what surprised you?

01:28:28.231 --> 01:28:33.490
If you share, offer a process question
rather than an identifiable incident.

01:28:33.491 --> 01:28:38.119
Check that you have separated observations
from interpretations and

01:28:38.120 --> 01:28:43.660
included what already helps. Where does
access depend on one person? Where could

01:28:43.661 --> 01:28:48.780
the process make it more dependable? These
questions may cross portfolios,

01:28:48.781 --> 01:28:53.760
including the front office, daily
organisation and the executive. Choose

01:28:53.761 --> 01:28:59.231
one routine or process to carry into the
design work, and keep both the map and

01:28:59.232 --> 01:29:00.282
audit with you.

01:29:01.800 --> 01:29:07.125
On page sixteen, or your Carry into design
card, choose a clear focus for the

01:29:07.126 --> 01:29:12.380
next session. For Keep, name one school
practice worth protecting because it

01:29:12.381 --> 01:29:17.846
supports access, connection or joy. For
Reduce, name one barrier or unnecessary

01:29:17.847 --> 01:29:23.032
demand. Then tick the category that best
fits your Reduce item: culture and

01:29:23.033 --> 01:29:28.148
learning, routines, or teams and
resourcing. Those categories will help us

01:29:28.149 --> 01:29:33.684
form trios in Session Four. For Explore,
write one question to take to students,

01:29:33.685 --> 01:29:39.430
families or staff. Keep the question open
if their knowledge is still needed. Allow

01:29:39.431 --> 01:29:44.896
about three minutes. Your responses can
stay private. Pause the video to finish

01:29:44.897 --> 01:29:48.890
the card, then keep it with your
school-day map and audit.

01:29:56.920 --> 01:30:01.676
Keep students first as you look at those
three choices. Design should protect what

01:30:01.677 --> 01:30:06.139
supports access, connection and joy as
well as reduce a barrier. Consultation

01:30:06.140 --> 01:30:10.661
can help us understand the questions still
open. We’ll take the map, the audit

01:30:10.662 --> 01:30:15.182
and your Reduce item into the learning
design session after lunch. In the live

01:30:15.183 --> 01:30:19.822
workshop, lunch runs from twelve thirty to
one. For this recording, you can take

01:30:19.823 --> 01:30:24.109
a break between sessions and return when
you’re ready. Before we close the

01:30:24.110 --> 01:30:28.395
morning, let’s come back to the fuller
picture of the students you’ve been

01:30:28.396 --> 01:30:29.335
holding in mind.

01:30:30.840 --> 01:30:35.168
Across Sessions Two and Three, you’ve been
building a picture of those

01:30:35.169 --> 01:30:39.998
students. How the world arrives through
their senses. How each thinks, focuses

01:30:39.999 --> 01:30:45.143
and builds understanding. Their strengths
and interests. Their internal experience.

01:30:45.144 --> 01:30:50.036
How they reach towards other people, and
what that can cost when the conditions

01:30:50.037 --> 01:30:54.804
don’t support them. Managing a school day
can involve sensory demands, social

01:30:54.805 --> 01:30:59.133
interpretation, executive effort and
communication across differences.

01:30:59.134 --> 01:31:03.712
Sometimes it also involves working to
appear fine when fine is not what is

01:31:03.713 --> 01:31:08.669
happening. That effort uses resources. A
child who knows who they are, and feels

01:31:08.670 --> 01:31:12.746
valued for it, has more of those resources
available for learning,

01:31:12.747 --> 01:31:17.075
connection and self-knowledge. The
research discussed in this workshop

01:31:17.076 --> 01:31:21.780
connects a positive sense of Autistic
identity with better mental health and

01:31:21.781 --> 01:31:26.486
wellbeing. Belonging matters. So does
agency: meaningful choice about how to

01:31:26.487 --> 01:31:31.191
communicate, where to be, and when to step
back. Knowing that other Autistic

01:31:31.192 --> 01:31:35.770
people share your experience can matter
too. There are Autistic adults and

01:31:35.771 --> 01:31:40.350
communities in which people find belonging
and purpose. These students are

01:31:40.351 --> 01:31:44.867
not alone. This belongs within our
educational work. We can make room for

01:31:44.868 --> 01:31:48.819
identity, connection and
self-understanding in the conditions we

01:31:48.820 --> 01:31:53.587
create every day. For leaders, belonging,
agency and connection belong in the

01:31:53.588 --> 01:31:58.293
school’s culture and routines, and in the
way staff are supported. Take that

01:31:58.294 --> 01:32:03.060
thought with you into the break. The
deepest thing you can do is support them

01:32:03.061 --> 01:32:07.201
to know themselves, and to know that who
they are is worth knowing.

01:32:17.240 --> 01:32:21.955
Welcome back. This is Learning Design.
Have your school-day map, school-level

01:32:21.956 --> 01:32:26.857
audit and Carry into design card beside
you. The summary is on page seventeen of

01:32:26.858 --> 01:32:31.387
your Participant Guide. We are moving from
what students experience to the

01:32:31.388 --> 01:32:36.164
systems a school creates. By the end of
this session, you will have one school

01:32:36.165 --> 01:32:41.066
change planned for this term, this year
and the strategic plan. We will consider

01:32:41.067 --> 01:32:46.155
culture, resourcing and the routines that
make access more dependable. Begin with a

01:32:46.156 --> 01:32:51.181
barrier or access question you noticed in
the morning. What we know about students

01:32:51.182 --> 01:32:56.145
helps us design, and what we do not yet
know remains a question for consultation.

01:32:56.146 --> 01:33:01.047
Keep your students’ strengths, interests
and ways of participating visible as we

01:33:01.048 --> 01:33:01.357
work.

01:33:02.880 --> 01:33:08.205
Students come first. Bring back the
different students you have in mind and

01:33:08.206 --> 01:33:13.820
the evidence on your map and audit. We
will design a change to a school system,

01:33:13.821 --> 01:33:18.930
then check that design against each of
those students. Before choosing a

01:33:18.931 --> 01:33:23.896
strategy, ask what you know about the
experience. What did the student

01:33:23.897 --> 01:33:29.295
communicate? What did someone observe?
What is still an interpretation or an

01:33:29.296 --> 01:33:34.909
unanswered question? The design response
follows that understanding. Your brief

01:33:34.910 --> 01:33:40.595
should connect something that matters for
students with something the school can

01:33:40.596 --> 01:33:45.849
influence in its systems, routines or
resourcing. It can remain open while

01:33:45.850 --> 01:33:51.032
students, families and staff add what they
know. We are making room for a

01:33:51.033 --> 01:33:56.285
fuller picture, rather than reducing a
student to a list of support needs.

01:33:57.800 --> 01:34:02.926
Our first leadership input is culture.
What staff say about students when they

01:34:02.927 --> 01:34:07.853
are not in the room is influenced by what
leaders model and allow to become

01:34:07.854 --> 01:34:12.847
familiar. Think about staffroom language.
If a student is described as being

01:34:12.848 --> 01:34:18.040
difficult, a leader might gently ask what
the lesson or the day is demanding of

01:34:18.041 --> 01:34:23.167
them. That is an illustrative response,
not a story about a particular school.

01:34:23.168 --> 01:34:28.560
Repeating that kind of question can make
curiosity part of ordinary practice. What

01:34:28.561 --> 01:34:33.754
gets celebrated also matters. Naming a
specific inclusive practice gives people

01:34:33.755 --> 01:34:38.947
something they can recognise and learn
from. For example, you might acknowledge

01:34:38.948 --> 01:34:44.074
a clear change-warning routine and the
access it supports, instead of offering

01:34:44.075 --> 01:34:48.935
only general praise. Now consider a vision
staff can say. Privately, write

01:34:48.936 --> 01:34:53.729
one sentence about inclusion that any
staff member could explain. Keep it

01:34:53.730 --> 01:34:58.523
connected to what students actually
experience. What would someone see or

01:34:58.524 --> 01:35:03.250
hear when that sentence is put into
practice? Remember Autistic staff as

01:35:03.251 --> 01:35:08.244
well. The culture should make it safer to
be oneself, with no expectation to

01:35:08.245 --> 01:35:13.171
disclose. Keep this reflection about
patterns and processes, not individual

01:35:13.172 --> 01:35:16.700
colleagues. Pause for a moment to write
your sentence.

01:35:24.720 --> 01:35:29.881
Read your sentence back. Does it help
staff know what inclusion asks of their

01:35:29.882 --> 01:35:35.043
practice? Keep it as a working version.
Students and staff who experience the

01:35:35.044 --> 01:35:39.797
culture need a place in reviewing it.
Leadership shapes culture through

01:35:39.798 --> 01:35:45.163
everyday language, what gets recognised
and what happens when someone asks for a

01:35:45.164 --> 01:35:46.452
practice to change.

01:35:47.960 --> 01:35:53.208
Our second input is resourcing. Where
money and staff time go expresses a

01:35:53.209 --> 01:35:58.675
school’s priorities. Think privately about
the question at the bottom of the

01:35:58.676 --> 01:36:04.143
slide: who decided the current support
staff allocation, and when? We do not

01:36:04.144 --> 01:36:09.465
need budget figures or identifiable
staffing arrangements here. The useful

01:36:09.466 --> 01:36:14.860
question is whether an allocation still
responds to need, or whether it has

01:36:14.861 --> 01:36:20.400
continued through habit. Your school-day
map and audit can help you ask where

01:36:20.401 --> 01:36:25.722
demand builds and where support is useful.
Also ask whether access depends

01:36:25.723 --> 01:36:30.971
on one scarce resource, such as one
support staff member, while the wider

01:36:30.972 --> 01:36:36.584
system remains difficult to use. Warning
time, a stable timetable and a useful

01:36:36.585 --> 01:36:42.489
template can sometimes reduce unnecessary
demand with little cost. They still need

01:36:42.490 --> 01:36:48.321
care, planning and review. Good records of
everyday practice support planning and

01:36:48.322 --> 01:36:53.424
the school’s N C C D and funding
processes. Follow your own governance,

01:36:53.425 --> 01:36:59.184
staffing and funding arrangements when
making formal decisions. For now, connect

01:36:59.185 --> 01:37:04.724
a resource question to what the map and
audit suggest about students’ access.

01:37:06.240 --> 01:37:11.093
The third input is leading change. Begin
with one system, explain why it matters,

01:37:11.094 --> 01:37:15.887
and support the people who carry the work
day to day. Plan the review before the

01:37:15.888 --> 01:37:20.680
change starts. Here are two illustrative
composites from the workshop materials.

01:37:20.681 --> 01:37:25.413
They are examples for discussion, not
accounts of real schools. In the first, a

01:37:25.414 --> 01:37:29.357
school starts with relief-day
arrangements. It agrees a relief-day

01:37:29.358 --> 01:37:33.848
plan, a way to warn students about a
change of teacher and a short handover

01:37:33.849 --> 01:37:38.338
note. Students and relief staff receive
clearer information, and the school

01:37:38.339 --> 01:37:43.192
reviews how it is working. In the second,
a school begins with a one-page student

01:37:43.193 --> 01:37:47.986
profile that travels between teachers,
year levels and relief staff. The purpose

01:37:47.987 --> 01:37:52.415
is to keep useful knowledge available when
people change. It still needs a

01:37:52.416 --> 01:37:56.177
clear process for keeping that information
current and using it

01:37:56.178 --> 01:38:01.092
appropriately. Which example is closer to
the next step your school needs? Perhaps

01:38:01.093 --> 01:38:05.643
neither answers your brief, but each shows
how one visible change can have a

01:38:05.644 --> 01:38:10.255
clear purpose. Middle leaders and staff
doing the work need time and support.

01:38:10.256 --> 01:38:14.927
What leaders do, including the time they
protect and the questions they ask at

01:38:14.928 --> 01:38:18.081
review, helps determine whether the change
continues.

01:38:19.600 --> 01:38:24.483
Here is the rhythm of the session. We have
begun with short inputs on culture,

01:38:24.484 --> 01:38:29.113
resourcing and change. We will regroup
into trios, write a brief, consider

01:38:29.114 --> 01:38:33.998
options, check accessibility and design a
school change. Then each person will

01:38:33.999 --> 01:38:38.818
privately check the design against their
own students, before we consider the

01:38:38.819 --> 01:38:43.575
continuum of support and finish with a
gallery and commitment. You will work

01:38:43.576 --> 01:38:48.713
together on one system while keeping your
own school and students in view. The end

01:38:48.714 --> 01:38:53.217
point is one change with a practical start
this term, something to build

01:38:53.218 --> 01:38:57.847
through the year and a place in the
strategic plan. The times on the slide

01:38:57.848 --> 01:39:02.668
describe the live workshop. In this
recording, pause for longer whenever your

01:39:02.669 --> 01:39:06.410
group needs working time, then resume to
hear the next step.

01:39:07.920 --> 01:39:12.511
Find the Reduce item on your Carry into
design card. Which system does it

01:39:12.512 --> 01:39:17.295
concern: culture and learning, routines,
or teams and resourcing? If you are

01:39:17.296 --> 01:39:21.952
together in a room, use those three
categories to form groups. Move to the

01:39:21.953 --> 01:39:27.054
area for the system you want to work on,
then form a trio with two people nearby.

01:39:27.055 --> 01:39:32.029
You can stay seated and arrange a group
around you if that works better. People

01:39:32.030 --> 01:39:37.068
from the same school may work together or
separately. Each trio needs the School

01:39:37.069 --> 01:39:41.852
change sheet. Its first page has the
shared design sections. The second page

01:39:41.853 --> 01:39:46.891
has a My students card for each person to
keep privately. You can also use pages

01:39:46.892 --> 01:39:52.058
eighteen to twenty-one of the Participant
Guide. If you are working independently,

01:39:52.059 --> 01:39:56.905
choose one system and follow the same
prompts, arranging a colleague’s review

01:39:56.906 --> 01:40:01.306
when you can. Allow a few minutes to
regroup. Keep your own school and

01:40:01.307 --> 01:40:06.154
students in mind as you work together.
Pause the video while you get settled.

01:40:14.160 --> 01:40:19.046
Let’s settle into the trios. Check that
you have the School change sheet or the

01:40:19.047 --> 01:40:23.619
matching Participant Guide pages, and that
each person has a place for the

01:40:23.620 --> 01:40:28.506
private student check. Your group will
design together, while each of you keeps

01:40:28.507 --> 01:40:30.135
your own students in view.

01:40:31.640 --> 01:40:36.827
Bring back a concise picture of each
student. Include strengths, interests and

01:40:36.828 --> 01:40:41.879
joy, communication, sensory experience and
thinking. Remember what costs and

01:40:41.880 --> 01:40:46.663
what lightens, and what you know about
identity and belonging. What have

01:40:46.664 --> 01:40:51.716
students, families and the team already
communicated? Which questions remain

01:40:51.717 --> 01:40:56.903
open? These are reminders, not another
form to complete. You are keeping real,

01:40:56.904 --> 01:41:02.427
different students in view as you design,
rather than building a file on one child.

01:41:02.428 --> 01:41:07.750
Later, each person will privately test the
shared design against their students.

01:41:07.751 --> 01:41:12.533
That is how we will notice where a
school-wide approach helps, and where

01:41:12.534 --> 01:41:14.689
someone may need something more.

01:41:16.200 --> 01:41:21.115
Agree on one school system your trio
recognises. It may come from one person’s

01:41:21.116 --> 01:41:26.286
Reduce item. You could choose relief-day
arrangements, how change is communicated,

01:41:26.287 --> 01:41:31.330
assemblies, where students can regulate,
or how useful information reaches their

01:41:31.331 --> 01:41:36.309
teachers. On section one of the School
change sheet, or page eighteen, complete

01:41:36.310 --> 01:41:41.289
three stems. At the moment, this system
asks students to. For some students and

01:41:41.290 --> 01:41:46.269
staff, that means. We could change. Keep
the first sentence concrete. What does

01:41:46.270 --> 01:41:51.121
the process actually ask a person to do,
understand or manage? In the second,

01:41:51.122 --> 01:41:55.462
distinguish what is known from what
remains a question. In the third,

01:41:55.463 --> 01:42:00.442
identify something within the school’s
influence. Read the wording together. Is

01:42:00.443 --> 01:42:05.485
it specific, workable and free from blame
or deficit labels? Describe the system

01:42:05.486 --> 01:42:10.465
rather than naming the people involved.
Keep this one brief active for the rest

01:42:10.466 --> 01:42:15.317
of the session. Allow about six minutes,
and pause the video while you write.

01:42:23.320 --> 01:42:27.651
Come back to your three sentences. Can you
point to the demand your brief

01:42:27.652 --> 01:42:32.164
describes? Does the proposed direction
respond to it? If a sentence explains

01:42:32.165 --> 01:42:36.856
what a student must be feeling without
their input, change it to an observation

01:42:36.857 --> 01:42:41.670
or a question. You have a brief to guide
your choices, with room for consultation

01:42:41.671 --> 01:42:42.452
to refine it.

01:42:43.960 --> 01:42:48.769
Keep that brief in front of you as we
consider four options. Professional

01:42:48.770 --> 01:42:53.512
learning and coaching can help staff
learning continue through the year.

01:42:53.513 --> 01:42:58.323
Predictability as policy concerns
relief-day plans, change warnings and a

01:42:58.324 --> 01:43:03.066
stable timetable. An inclusion team can
plan and review adjustments on a

01:43:03.067 --> 01:43:08.010
schedule. Useful documentation can help
knowledge travel through templates,

01:43:08.011 --> 01:43:13.087
profiles and a resource bank. Listen for
what might answer the demand in your

01:43:13.088 --> 01:43:18.565
brief. You will choose one or two options
after these short inputs. The headings on

01:43:18.566 --> 01:43:23.976
this slide and section two of your School
change sheet are available to work from.

01:43:23.977 --> 01:43:29.321
If your group has printed option cards,
you can use those to sort your choices as

01:43:29.322 --> 01:43:29.655
well.

01:43:31.160 --> 01:43:35.877
Professional learning and coaching asks
what happens after a day like today.

01:43:35.878 --> 01:43:40.658
Where can staff ask questions, observe
practice, try something and reflect on

01:43:40.659 --> 01:43:45.376
what students experience? The slide
proposes learning that continues through

01:43:45.377 --> 01:43:49.780
the year, coaching and mentoring in
classrooms, time to plan and review

01:43:49.781 --> 01:43:54.750
together, and a mentor for every early
career teacher. The structure will differ

01:43:54.751 --> 01:43:59.594
across schools. What matters is making a
place for the learning to continue in

01:43:59.595 --> 01:44:03.934
practice. For a primary example, a teacher
might invite a colleague to

01:44:03.935 --> 01:44:08.841
observe a change-warning routine, then
review it together at a team meeting. In

01:44:08.842 --> 01:44:13.811
a secondary example, a faculty might use
part of a meeting for staff to share an

01:44:13.812 --> 01:44:18.466
adjustment they tried and what they
noticed. These are illustrations, not a

01:44:18.467 --> 01:44:23.122
model everyone must adopt. How would
student and family feedback enter that

01:44:23.123 --> 01:44:27.714
review? What time would need to be
protected? Keep the option connected to

01:44:27.715 --> 01:44:32.621
the demand in your brief, so professional
learning has a purpose that staff can

01:44:32.622 --> 01:44:33.250
recognise.

01:44:34.760 --> 01:44:40.048
Predictability includes clear information
about routine and change. It does not

01:44:40.049 --> 01:44:45.270
require every day to be identical. Ask
whether every student, and every relief

01:44:45.271 --> 01:44:50.423
teacher, can answer four questions. What
is happening? What is changing? What

01:44:50.424 --> 01:44:55.373
comes next? What can I do if the plan
changes? Consider a primary assembly

01:44:55.374 --> 01:45:00.459
moved earlier than usual. How will
students learn that the plan has changed?

01:45:00.460 --> 01:45:05.138
Is the change reflected in the information
they use, and can they find

01:45:05.139 --> 01:45:09.952
out what happens next? In a secondary
example, a class has both a relief

01:45:09.953 --> 01:45:15.241
teacher and a room change. Is there a
dependable way for students to learn that

01:45:15.242 --> 01:45:20.531
before they arrive, and does the relief
teacher have what they need? Relief-day

01:45:20.532 --> 01:45:25.684
plans, warning arrangements and timetable
stability involve school decisions.

01:45:25.685 --> 01:45:30.906
Return to your audit: when something
changes, how do students find out? Is the

01:45:30.907 --> 01:45:36.059
information accessible when it matters, or
does a person need to know whom to

01:45:36.060 --> 01:45:41.280
ask? Your design could make that route
clearer. Test it on an ordinary day and

01:45:41.281 --> 01:45:45.348
on a day when plans change, using student
and staff feedback.

01:45:46.880 --> 01:45:51.751
An inclusion team and a review cycle can
make adjustments more dependable. The

01:45:51.752 --> 01:45:56.306
idea is a small team that plans and
reviews on a schedule, so review also

01:45:56.307 --> 01:46:00.924
happens before something goes wrong. There
are four decisions to make. Who

01:46:00.925 --> 01:46:05.606
needs to be part of the team? How often
will it meet? How will students and

01:46:05.607 --> 01:46:10.161
families contribute? How will decisions
reach every teacher who needs the

01:46:10.162 --> 01:46:15.159
information, including relief staff? The
membership and frequency will depend on

01:46:15.160 --> 01:46:19.714
your school and system. Start with the
question on the slide: who reviews

01:46:19.715 --> 01:46:24.333
adjustments now, and how often? What is
already clear, and what depends on

01:46:24.334 --> 01:46:28.888
someone remembering to ask? Review can
confirm that an adjustment remains

01:46:28.889 --> 01:46:33.823
useful, suggest a change or show that a
particular support is no longer needed.

01:46:33.824 --> 01:46:38.504
Base that judgement on experience,
consultation and evidence. A schedule is

01:46:38.505 --> 01:46:43.629
useful when it creates a real opportunity
to hear what is happening and act on it.

01:46:45.160 --> 01:46:50.853
Documentation should help staff act on
what is known. Think about profiles that

01:46:50.854 --> 01:46:56.840
travel, adjustment records, templates and
a resource bank people will actually use.

01:46:56.841 --> 01:47:02.388
Keep the purpose practical. Can staff find
the information when they need it?

01:47:02.389 --> 01:47:07.645
Is it short enough to use? Does it reflect
what is happening now? Does it

01:47:07.646 --> 01:47:13.193
reach relief teachers? Page nineteen has
these quick-review questions if your

01:47:13.194 --> 01:47:18.888
change involves documentation. A one-page
profile may be useful, but its length

01:47:18.889 --> 01:47:24.436
alone does not make it effective. Someone
needs to know where it belongs, who

01:47:24.437 --> 01:47:29.839
updates it and how it reaches the
appropriate people. The same is true of a

01:47:29.840 --> 01:47:34.803
resource bank: availability and usefulness
need checking in practice.

01:47:34.804 --> 01:47:40.498
Connect your design with the school’s
existing privacy, recordkeeping and N C C

01:47:40.499 --> 01:47:45.608
D processes. We are looking for continuity
and access, with information

01:47:45.609 --> 01:47:51.303
kept current and shared appropriately.
Keep asking what this record will help a

01:47:51.304 --> 01:47:54.587
staff member understand or do for the
student.

01:47:56.120 --> 01:48:01.186
Return to your brief. Which one or two
options answer it? You have heard about

01:48:01.187 --> 01:48:06.186
professional learning and coaching,
predictability as policy, inclusion teams

01:48:06.187 --> 01:48:11.253
and review cycles, and documentation that
helps staff. Use the headings on the

01:48:11.254 --> 01:48:16.583
sheet, or sort the printed cards if your
group has them. Choose one or two, rather

01:48:16.584 --> 01:48:21.715
than collecting all four. Something else
is also available on the sheet if your

01:48:21.716 --> 01:48:26.913
brief needs a different response. Record
the choice in section two of the School

01:48:26.914 --> 01:48:31.980
change sheet, or page eighteen. Beside it,
explain which demand in your school

01:48:31.981 --> 01:48:37.112
it addresses. That reason matters more
than how familiar or appealing an option

01:48:37.113 --> 01:48:42.047
sounds. Allow about two minutes. Pause the
video while your trio chooses and

01:48:42.048 --> 01:48:42.837
records why.

01:48:50.840 --> 01:48:55.820
Let’s come back. Read the reason beside
each option. Can another person see the

01:48:55.821 --> 01:49:00.737
connection between the demand and the
response? If the reason is only that the

01:49:00.738 --> 01:49:05.909
option is generally useful, make it more
specific. Keep the choice manageable, and

01:49:05.910 --> 01:49:08.719
keep any unanswered question beside the
plan.

01:49:10.240 --> 01:49:15.248
Before you finish the design, let’s apply
a Universal Design for Learning lens. Can

01:49:15.249 --> 01:49:20.196
you make important information clear and
available? Can you offer workable ways to

01:49:20.197 --> 01:49:24.778
participate and respond? Can you support
engagement while keeping meaningful

01:49:24.779 --> 01:49:29.604
challenge in the learning? Knowledge of
individual students and universal design

01:49:29.605 --> 01:49:34.307
can inform each other. Something you
understand about one student’s access may

01:49:34.308 --> 01:49:38.950
reveal a barrier worth reducing more
broadly. A broadly available support may

01:49:38.951 --> 01:49:43.532
then help several students in different
ways. But the presence of a resource

01:49:43.533 --> 01:49:48.113
doesn’t guarantee access. Offering another
format only helps if students can

01:49:48.114 --> 01:49:52.817
find it, understand it and use it. We’ll
now examine your emerging design with

01:49:52.818 --> 01:49:57.276
four practical questions, then test it
through use and feedback. At school

01:49:57.277 --> 01:50:01.552
level, this can shape policies, templates,
timetables and shared spaces

01:50:01.553 --> 01:50:06.317
as well as individual lessons. Keep that
wider application in view as you check

01:50:06.318 --> 01:50:06.989
the design.

01:50:08.520 --> 01:50:13.896
Use section three of your A three sheet,
with the prompts on page nineteen. Run

01:50:13.897 --> 01:50:19.066
four questions over your draft design. Can
students find and understand what

01:50:19.067 --> 01:50:24.167
they need? Can they take part in more than
one way? Can they pause, ask for

01:50:24.168 --> 01:50:29.406
help or recover? Is the support available
when demand increases? Consider the

01:50:29.407 --> 01:50:34.300
details. If help is only available by
asking aloud, is that workable for

01:50:34.301 --> 01:50:39.057
everyone using this routine? If
information is useful at the start but

01:50:39.058 --> 01:50:44.572
disappears during a transition, will it be
there when needed? These are questions

01:50:44.573 --> 01:50:50.017
to test against your setting, rather than
assumptions about a student. Tick each

01:50:50.018 --> 01:50:55.256
question as you consider it. Where the
answer is ‘not yet’, adjust the design

01:50:55.257 --> 01:50:59.875
before moving on. Allow about four
minutes. Also consider a day when

01:50:59.876 --> 01:51:05.114
something changes. Would students across
the school still be able to find the

01:51:05.115 --> 01:51:10.215
information and support your design
offers? Pause the video while your trio

01:51:10.216 --> 01:51:12.559
completes the accessibility check.

01:51:20.560 --> 01:51:25.352
Come back to the changes you made. The
check should influence the design, not

01:51:25.353 --> 01:51:29.389
just record that you discussed
accessibility. Keep any unresolved

01:51:29.390 --> 01:51:34.182
question beside the plan. This is an
initial check. You’ll still need to find

01:51:34.183 --> 01:51:39.101
out what happens when students use the
support and have a way to give feedback.

01:51:39.102 --> 01:51:43.579
Access is demonstrated through experience,
rather than guaranteed by the

01:51:43.580 --> 01:51:45.219
resource we have prepared.

01:51:46.720 --> 01:51:51.805
Now develop the school change on section
four of your A three sheet, or page

01:51:51.806 --> 01:51:57.094
twenty. Work through the three timeframes
first. This term: what decision could

01:51:57.095 --> 01:52:01.638
cost little and start soon? It might be an
agreed relief-day plan, a

01:52:01.639 --> 01:52:06.249
change-warning arrangement or a leadership
practice at the next staff

01:52:06.250 --> 01:52:11.403
meeting. This year: what will you build?
Perhaps a useful profile template, a

01:52:11.404 --> 01:52:16.625
review cycle or a coaching arrangement.
The strategic plan: what will help the

01:52:16.626 --> 01:52:21.711
change last when staff or roles change?
That could include a goal and review

01:52:21.712 --> 01:52:27.000
point in the school plan, or induction
that explains the shared approach. These

01:52:27.001 --> 01:52:32.154
are examples to help you think, not a list
to copy. Then decide what you will

01:52:32.155 --> 01:52:37.647
observe and when you will review it. Look
for evidence about access, participation

01:52:37.648 --> 01:52:42.869
and experience. A quieter room or more
compliant behaviour alone will not tell

01:52:42.870 --> 01:52:48.294
you whether the change is helping. Near
the end, ask a neighbouring trio to check

01:52:48.295 --> 01:52:53.583
that your design remains student-centred
and focused on the environment. If you

01:52:53.584 --> 01:52:58.601
are working independently, arrange that
check with a colleague. Allow about

01:52:58.602 --> 01:53:03.484
twelve minutes for the design and review.
Pause the video while you work.

01:53:11.520 --> 01:53:15.817
Let’s return. You should be able to
describe the first step, what you intend

01:53:15.818 --> 01:53:20.231
to build and how the change could become
part of the school’s continuing work.

01:53:20.232 --> 01:53:24.873
Check that you have something to observe
and a review point. If your trio includes

01:53:24.874 --> 01:53:28.942
people from different schools, each person
can adapt the design to their

01:53:28.943 --> 01:53:33.470
setting. Keep its purpose visible while
you do. The next check brings us back to

01:53:33.471 --> 01:53:37.711
your different students and what the same
design may mean for each of them.

01:53:39.240 --> 01:53:43.734
This next part is private. Take your own
My students card and turn to page

01:53:43.735 --> 01:53:48.105
twenty-one if you’re using the Participant
Guide. Use Student A, Student

01:53:48.106 --> 01:53:52.661
B and Student C, without names or
identifying details. Consider your trio’s

01:53:52.662 --> 01:53:57.525
design against each student. Mark Helps,
No difference, or Needs something more.

01:53:57.526 --> 01:54:02.143
Treat this as a working judgement based on
what you know, and keep questions

01:54:02.144 --> 01:54:06.699
visible where you need the student’s or
family’s perspective. Between three

01:54:06.700 --> 01:54:11.439
people, a trio may be holding six to nine
different students in view. The same

01:54:11.440 --> 01:54:16.365
design may work differently for each. You
can share only what you choose, perhaps

01:54:16.366 --> 01:54:21.229
how many Needs something more marks the
design collected, without describing the

01:54:21.230 --> 01:54:25.723
students. Allow about five minutes for
your private check and any optional

01:54:25.724 --> 01:54:28.186
sharing. Pause the video while you work.

01:54:36.200 --> 01:54:41.004
Let’s return to the shared design. Where
it helps, the universal approach is doing

01:54:41.005 --> 01:54:44.919
useful work. Where it makes no difference,
or where a student needs

01:54:44.920 --> 01:54:49.664
something more, we have an important next
question. That doesn’t make the student

01:54:49.665 --> 01:54:54.410
the problem, and it doesn’t necessarily
make the whole design a failure. It shows

01:54:54.411 --> 01:54:58.681
us where targeted support or an individual
adjustment may be needed. Keep

01:54:58.682 --> 01:55:03.367
those Needs something more marks in mind
as we look at the continuum of support.

01:55:04.880 --> 01:55:09.678
Support is a continuum, rather than one
intervention that answers every need.

01:55:09.679 --> 01:55:14.540
Start with an inclusive environment:
predictable routines, clear expectations,

01:55:14.541 --> 01:55:18.960
accessible information, and a classroom
culture where different ways of

01:55:18.961 --> 01:55:23.380
participating are accepted and valued.
Then consider universal supports

01:55:23.381 --> 01:55:28.243
available to many students. Instructions
can be offered in spoken, written and

01:55:28.244 --> 01:55:32.915
visual forms. Students may have flexible
ways to demonstrate learning, with

01:55:32.916 --> 01:55:37.399
movement breaks or sensory tools available
when useful. Targeted support

01:55:37.400 --> 01:55:42.324
responds to a more specific need. For
example, a small group might benefit from

01:55:42.325 --> 01:55:47.312
key vocabulary being introduced before a
lesson, or from additional organisation

01:55:47.313 --> 01:55:51.669
support during independent work.
Individual adjustments respond to the

01:55:51.670 --> 01:55:55.837
particular student’s access and
participation needs. Examples might

01:55:55.838 --> 01:56:00.573
include a quieter workspace for an
assessment, additional processing time, a

01:56:00.574 --> 01:56:05.245
personalised task breakdown or another way
to demonstrate learning. What is

01:56:05.246 --> 01:56:10.234
appropriate depends on the student and the
context, informed by consultation and

01:56:10.235 --> 01:56:15.349
evidence. Look back at the design check.
What is available to everyone? What might

01:56:15.350 --> 01:56:19.264
need to be targeted? What needs individual
adjustment? Academic

01:56:19.265 --> 01:56:23.810
adjustments sit within this wider picture.
A diagnosis alone doesn’t tell

01:56:23.811 --> 01:56:28.735
us which adjustment will help. Connect the
response with your observations, the

01:56:28.736 --> 01:56:33.282
day map, and student and family knowledge,
then review it through use and

01:56:33.283 --> 01:56:37.891
feedback. For each layer, ask who decides,
who delivers and who reviews in

01:56:37.892 --> 01:56:42.438
your school. A useful continuum also needs
clear responsibilities, so the

01:56:42.439 --> 01:56:46.416
support reaches students and can be
adjusted when their needs or

01:56:46.417 --> 01:56:47.742
circumstances change.

01:56:49.280 --> 01:56:54.268
Reasonable adjustments are changes or
supports that help students access and

01:56:54.269 --> 01:56:58.791
participate in education in a way that
works for them. They should be

01:56:58.792 --> 01:57:03.580
individualised, build on strengths, remove
barriers, and be practical and

01:57:03.581 --> 01:57:08.436
effective. Informed decisions and review
help us find out whether they are

01:57:08.437 --> 01:57:13.491
enabling the student. The diagram places
adjustments within the wider work of

01:57:13.492 --> 01:57:18.879
environmental design and Universal Design
for Learning. It also names the four N C

01:57:18.880 --> 01:57:23.269
C D levels of adjustment: quality
differentiated teaching practice,

01:57:23.270 --> 01:57:28.391
supplementary, substantial and extensive.
Keep two things distinct. Reasonable

01:57:28.392 --> 01:57:33.713
adjustments are the changes and supports
provided. The N C C D is the process for

01:57:33.714 --> 01:57:39.101
collecting information about adjustments
for students with disability. Evidence of

01:57:39.102 --> 01:57:43.956
what has been provided and monitored
matters; a diagnostic label by itself

01:57:43.957 --> 01:57:49.278
doesn’t choose an adjustment or tell the
whole story. Within curriculum learning,

01:57:49.279 --> 01:57:53.601
we want support to build capability,
confidence, self-advocacy and

01:57:53.602 --> 01:57:58.790
independence. The question is whether
students can access and participate, with

01:57:58.791 --> 01:58:03.912
support that empowers them. For formal
requirements and decisions, use current

01:58:03.913 --> 01:58:07.437
official guidance and your school or
system processes.

01:58:08.960 --> 01:58:13.535
Good practice creates useful evidence:
consultation records, documented

01:58:13.536 --> 01:58:18.307
adjustments and a cycle of review. Leaders
influence what staff gather and

01:58:18.308 --> 01:58:23.079
how easy it is to record. Ask what
evidence you currently request. Does it

01:58:23.080 --> 01:58:28.178
help explain a student’s access needs,
what has been provided and what has been

01:58:28.179 --> 01:58:33.146
learned through review? Can staff record
it as part of their work, or has the

01:58:33.147 --> 01:58:38.049
process become hard to maintain? Connect
this with the documentation option:

01:58:38.050 --> 01:58:42.886
short, current templates can help
information remain useful. For formal N C

01:58:42.887 --> 01:58:47.593
C D requirements, use current official
guidance and follow your school or

01:58:47.594 --> 01:58:52.757
system’s processes. Keep the educational
access question in view. Records should

01:58:52.758 --> 01:58:57.072
support informed decisions and continuity,
while student and family

01:58:57.073 --> 01:59:02.432
knowledge continues to shape the work. In
Session Five we will develop consultation

01:59:02.433 --> 01:59:07.727
and partnership further, including the N C
C D Meetings Tool Kit. Keep any process

01:59:07.728 --> 01:59:12.303
questions beside your design so you can
take them into that discussion.

01:59:13.840 --> 01:59:18.563
It’s time to see what other trios have
designed. Put your A three sheet on the

01:59:18.564 --> 01:59:23.287
wall, checking first that it contains no
student names or identifying details.

01:59:23.288 --> 01:59:28.072
Walk through the gallery and read a few
other designs. On another trio’s sheet,

01:59:28.073 --> 01:59:32.489
leave a Post-it with one idea you would
borrow or one question. Keep your

01:59:32.490 --> 01:59:37.091
question curious and useful: something
that might help the group clarify the

01:59:37.092 --> 01:59:41.753
access question or refine the change. Then
return to your own design and read

01:59:41.754 --> 01:59:46.048
the notes others have left. Allow about
five minutes. If you’re working

01:59:46.049 --> 01:59:50.710
independently, use this point to ask a
colleague for that same brief response

01:59:50.711 --> 01:59:53.778
when you can. Pause the video for the
gallery walk.

02:00:01.800 --> 02:00:06.738
Come back to your own design. Read the
feedback and consider whether you want to

02:00:06.739 --> 02:00:11.551
refine anything. You don’t need to adopt
every suggestion; test it against the

02:00:11.552 --> 02:00:16.115
brief and the students’ access. The
gallery lets useful ideas travel, much

02:00:16.116 --> 02:00:20.804
like our communication wall in Session
Three. An idea from another group can

02:00:20.805 --> 02:00:25.680
open a possibility, while still needing to
fit the students and routine in your

02:00:25.681 --> 02:00:26.180
setting.

02:00:27.680 --> 02:00:33.420
Use the Commit space on page twenty-one,
or the bottom of your My students card.

02:00:33.421 --> 02:00:39.088
What will you start this term? What will
you notice? Who on your executive will

02:00:39.089 --> 02:00:44.829
you share it with? How will students and
families help refine it? Choose a first

02:00:44.830 --> 02:00:50.570
step within your influence. It may be a
conversation before a trial, rather than

02:00:50.571 --> 02:00:55.875
an announcement that the design is
finished. The people affected need room

02:00:55.876 --> 02:01:01.688
to shape what happens. Write the step and
the colleague or role you will involve.

02:01:01.689 --> 02:01:06.848
Keep the evidence and review point visible
as well. You may share with a

02:01:06.849 --> 02:01:12.225
partner if you choose, keeping public
examples anonymised. Allow about four

02:01:12.226 --> 02:01:16.440
minutes, and pause the video while you
make the commitment.

02:01:24.480 --> 02:01:29.172
Keep that commitment with your School
change sheet. You now have a system you

02:01:29.173 --> 02:01:34.050
have examined, a change planned across
three timeframes and a next step that can

02:01:34.051 --> 02:01:38.558
involve other people’s knowledge. Treat it
as something to try, review and

02:01:38.559 --> 02:01:43.313
refine. Students, families or staff may
help you notice something the original

02:01:43.314 --> 02:01:48.252
map missed, or show that a support works
differently from what you expected. That

02:01:48.253 --> 02:01:53.192
information belongs in the next version.
Your this-term step can become the Start

02:01:53.193 --> 02:01:57.391
on your action card in Session Six. First,
we will turn to Working in

02:01:57.392 --> 02:02:02.023
Partnership and the leadership practices
that help student, family and staff

02:02:02.024 --> 02:02:03.628
knowledge shape decisions.

02:02:11.640 --> 02:02:16.153
Welcome to Working in Partnership. In the
last session you designed a school

02:02:16.154 --> 02:02:20.787
change. Now we’ll consider how students,
families and staff can understand the

02:02:20.788 --> 02:02:25.120
experience together and shape what happens
next. Look at your My students

02:02:25.121 --> 02:02:29.574
card and the Commit space on page
twenty-one. You wrote who you would share

02:02:29.575 --> 02:02:33.907
the change with, including an executive
colleague. Leaders also shape the

02:02:33.908 --> 02:02:38.300
routines in which contact, meetings,
decisions and follow-up happen. Those

02:02:38.301 --> 02:02:42.574
routines are part of our work here. You
may bring productive partnership

02:02:42.575 --> 02:02:47.027
experiences and difficult ones, with
families or within your own team. Keep

02:02:47.028 --> 02:02:51.481
examples anonymised, use roles or
initials, and share only what you choose.

02:02:51.482 --> 02:02:55.754
The student stays at the centre.
Partnership begins while we develop our

02:02:55.755 --> 02:02:59.425
understanding and continues through
design, action and review.

02:03:00.960 --> 02:03:05.867
Look at the overlapping circles. Family,
school staff and paraprofessionals each

02:03:05.868 --> 02:03:09.906
bring knowledge and a different
relationship with the student. The

02:03:09.907 --> 02:03:14.814
student is right at the centre. A family
may understand something about recovery

02:03:14.815 --> 02:03:19.536
after school that we don't see in the
classroom. An educator may notice how a

02:03:19.537 --> 02:03:24.320
particular task changes participation. A
support professional may contribute a

02:03:24.321 --> 02:03:29.229
different perspective again. Those are
possible contributions, rather than fixed

02:03:29.230 --> 02:03:33.205
roles. No one person has the whole
picture, including us. On page

02:03:33.206 --> 02:03:37.492
twenty-two, you'll find Where partnerships
converge. With someone near

02:03:37.493 --> 02:03:42.463
you, consider this question: what is the
goal of these intersecting partnerships?

02:03:42.464 --> 02:03:47.495
Then ask, who might be missing from this
picture? How will the student communicate

02:03:47.496 --> 02:03:52.342
their experience and preferences? Think
back to sensory and thinking in Session

02:03:52.343 --> 02:03:56.877
Two, communication, connection and
identity in Session Three, and learning

02:03:56.878 --> 02:04:01.164
design and the continuum of support in
Session Four. What should these

02:04:01.165 --> 02:04:05.638
partnerships make possible for the
student? Pause the video if you'd like

02:04:05.639 --> 02:04:08.993
longer to talk, and then we'll draw the
ideas together.

02:04:17.000 --> 02:04:21.612
Let's come back to the student at the
centre. A useful shared purpose connects

02:04:21.613 --> 02:04:25.865
our work to their learning, access,
participation and experience. Having

02:04:25.866 --> 02:04:29.639
several people involved doesn't
automatically make a partnership

02:04:29.640 --> 02:04:34.133
effective. Their knowledge needs a way
into the decisions. If you identified

02:04:34.134 --> 02:04:38.446
someone missing, keep that in mind. If the
student's perspective is being

02:04:38.447 --> 02:04:43.238
inferred entirely by adults, consider how
you could check that understanding with

02:04:43.239 --> 02:04:47.612
them. The circles are prompts for thinking
about relationships. They can't

02:04:47.613 --> 02:04:52.105
capture every person or every kind of
knowledge that matters to a particular

02:04:52.106 --> 02:04:56.598
student. Look at your school’s processes.
Where do they bring these partners

02:04:56.599 --> 02:05:01.450
together, and where does useful knowledge
remain separate? Leadership can create a

02:05:01.451 --> 02:05:04.925
dependable route for that knowledge to
influence decisions.

02:05:06.440 --> 02:05:11.113
These six principles come from the
Family-School Partnerships Framework.

02:05:11.114 --> 02:05:15.589
Let's connect each one to what we do.
First, parents and families are

02:05:15.590 --> 02:05:19.867
children's first and continuing educators.
Their knowledge remains

02:05:19.868 --> 02:05:24.870
valuable when a child enters school.
Second, learning is lifelong and happens

02:05:24.871 --> 02:05:30.267
across many settings. The classroom gives
us an important view, but it is one view.

02:05:30.268 --> 02:05:35.533
Third, partnerships are strengthened when
the diversity and strengths of families

02:05:35.534 --> 02:05:40.799
are valued. We need to learn what those
strengths are, rather than assuming every

02:05:40.800 --> 02:05:45.868
family can contribute in the same way.
Fourth, community engagement can expand

02:05:45.869 --> 02:05:50.871
the people, knowledge and resources
involved in learning. Fifth, partnerships

02:05:50.872 --> 02:05:55.544
grow through mutual trust, respect and
responsibility. We can show those

02:05:55.545 --> 02:06:00.086
qualities by listening carefully,
explaining clearly and doing what we

02:06:00.087 --> 02:06:04.957
agree to do. Sixth, partnership needs
committed, collaborative and creative

02:06:04.958 --> 02:06:09.565
leadership. Someone needs to make the time
and processes possible. In a

02:06:09.566 --> 02:06:14.370
meeting, these principles become visible
through a shared purpose, student

02:06:14.371 --> 02:06:19.438
communication, accessible information, and
clear next steps. Who will do what,

02:06:19.439 --> 02:06:24.507
and when will we check in again? Family
and school perspectives may differ. We

02:06:24.508 --> 02:06:29.773
can listen and show respect while we are
still working through those differences.

02:06:29.774 --> 02:06:35.171
Bringing those perspectives together from
the beginning gives us a better basis for

02:06:35.172 --> 02:06:40.042
deciding what to try. Pay particular
attention to the final principle about

02:06:40.043 --> 02:06:44.649
leadership. What can you make possible
through time, meeting design and

02:06:44.650 --> 02:06:49.257
follow-up, so these principles are visible
in ordinary school practice?

02:06:50.760 --> 02:06:55.789
There are many points of collaboration
around an Autistic student: between

02:06:55.790 --> 02:07:01.164
teachers, with a family, with support
staff, with other professionals, and with

02:07:01.165 --> 02:07:06.332
the student themselves. Across those
relationships, the core skills are much

02:07:06.333 --> 02:07:11.086
the same. Before we work through the ideas
on the slide, think about a

02:07:11.087 --> 02:07:16.598
collaboration that helped you understand
something better. What made it possible?

02:07:16.599 --> 02:07:21.835
Perhaps there was a clear purpose, time to
ask questions, or a willingness to

02:07:21.836 --> 02:07:27.003
change an initial view. At your table,
name two or three principles that you

02:07:27.004 --> 02:07:32.447
think matter most. You can also consider
the question on the slide: how could we

02:07:32.448 --> 02:07:37.063
work together for better learning
outcomes? Keep the student in that

02:07:37.064 --> 02:07:41.610
conversation. Pause the video for a longer
discussion, then resume.

02:07:49.640 --> 02:07:54.252
Let's bring those principles together.
Shared goals help us understand what

02:07:54.253 --> 02:07:59.302
we're working towards, and shared values
help us understand why it matters. Mutual

02:07:59.303 --> 02:08:04.164
trust and respect need to be present in
the way we speak and listen. Consulting

02:08:04.165 --> 02:08:09.214
on decisions means leaving room for what
we hear to influence the decision. Seeing

02:08:09.215 --> 02:08:13.515
another perspective and inviting curiosity
help us notice what our own

02:08:13.516 --> 02:08:18.191
position might hide. Centring student
voice reminds us whose experience this

02:08:18.192 --> 02:08:22.991
work concerns. Assuming good intent gives
us a constructive starting point. We

02:08:22.992 --> 02:08:27.604
still need to attend to the effects of our
actions, and to concerns someone

02:08:27.605 --> 02:08:32.716
raises. A useful question is, what can we
learn from each other that will help this

02:08:32.717 --> 02:08:37.516
student? These principles apply whoever is
at the table. They apply to leaders

02:08:37.517 --> 02:08:42.379
working with their own staff as well as to
staff working with families. The way

02:08:42.380 --> 02:08:46.992
we consult within the school helps
establish what collaboration looks like.

02:08:48.520 --> 02:08:53.392
Family engagement can be designed into
school routines. A family’s experience

02:08:53.393 --> 02:08:58.393
should be dependable when their child
changes teacher or when staff move roles.

02:08:58.394 --> 02:09:03.074
Start with first contact. The slide
proposes making the first contact each

02:09:03.075 --> 02:09:08.139
term a positive one, so a family hears
what is valued about their child before a

02:09:08.140 --> 02:09:12.756
concern arises. Then consider meeting
protocols, shaped with families. An

02:09:12.757 --> 02:09:17.565
agenda in advance, room for a support
person, time for questions and a short

02:09:17.566 --> 02:09:22.630
written summary can all make a meeting
more accessible. Communication also needs

02:09:22.631 --> 02:09:27.759
continuity. Agreed channels, a record of
communication preferences and a handover

02:09:27.760 --> 02:09:32.440
when a teacher or case manager changes can
help the relationship continue.

02:09:32.441 --> 02:09:37.441
Access includes interpreters, different
meeting formats and flexible times. Ask

02:09:37.442 --> 02:09:42.186
what is workable for the particular
family. Which of these happens reliably

02:09:42.187 --> 02:09:46.738
in your school? Which depends on
particular staff? Treat that dependency

02:09:46.739 --> 02:09:51.996
as a question about the process. There is
no need to name or blame anyone. Finally,

02:09:51.997 --> 02:09:57.190
ask families whether they feel heard, and
act on what they tell you. That might be

02:09:57.191 --> 02:10:02.127
a question at the end of a meeting or a
follow-up conversation. Tell them what

02:10:02.128 --> 02:10:07.192
changed as a result. A family-engagement
routine may belong in the school change

02:10:07.193 --> 02:10:08.859
you began in Session Four.

02:10:10.400 --> 02:10:15.419
Sometimes trust has already broken down. A
leader may be able to help reset a

02:10:15.420 --> 02:10:20.440
relationship that a teacher cannot repair
alone. Families may carry difficult

02:10:20.441 --> 02:10:25.592
experiences from earlier years or other
schools. Listen to that history without

02:10:25.593 --> 02:10:30.943
deciding in advance what it means. Begin
by acknowledging what went wrong in plain

02:10:30.944 --> 02:10:35.765
language. Hear the family’s account before
offering solutions or defending

02:10:35.766 --> 02:10:40.785
the process. Then agree a clear next step,
who is responsible and a follow-up

02:10:40.786 --> 02:10:46.004
date. Trust depends on what happens after
the meeting as well as what is said in

02:10:46.005 --> 02:10:50.892
it. Think privately about what a school
can acknowledge, clarify and change

02:10:50.893 --> 02:10:56.176
within its authority. What follow-through
is possible? We cannot promise that one

02:10:56.177 --> 02:11:01.131
conversation will restore trust or
guarantee a particular outcome. We can be

02:11:01.132 --> 02:11:06.085
clear about the next step we are able to
take and then take it. Keep current

02:11:06.086 --> 02:11:11.105
disputes and identifying details out of
this reflection. Local complaints and

02:11:11.106 --> 02:11:16.192
escalation processes still apply. The
focus here is the part a leader can play

02:11:16.193 --> 02:11:21.146
through listening, acknowledgement and
reliable follow-through. The everyday

02:11:21.147 --> 02:11:25.770
engagement practices on the previous slide
give that work a foundation.

02:11:27.280 --> 02:11:32.244
The seesaw invites us to notice power
between school, home and the student.

02:11:32.245 --> 02:11:37.343
Power can sit in income and resources,
past experiences of education, culture

02:11:37.344 --> 02:11:42.376
and language, or knowledge of school
systems and curriculum. It can also sit

02:11:42.377 --> 02:11:47.475
in what each person believes about school
and professional authority. We work

02:11:47.476 --> 02:11:52.641
within education every day. Its language
and routines can feel ordinary to us,

02:11:52.642 --> 02:11:58.076
while being unfamiliar or intimidating to
someone else. Think about an agenda full

02:11:58.077 --> 02:12:03.243
of acronyms, a meeting time that is hard
to attend, or a decision presented as

02:12:03.244 --> 02:12:08.610
already settled. Ask yourself: who sets
the agenda? Whose knowledge is heard? Who

02:12:08.611 --> 02:12:13.441
can make decisions? Is the student
included in a way that works for them?

02:12:13.442 --> 02:12:18.406
And what could the school change about the
process? These questions help us

02:12:18.407 --> 02:12:22.701
examine practice. They aren't an
invitation to assign blame to an

02:12:22.702 --> 02:12:28.135
individual. Before describing a family as
difficult or disengaged, we need to look

02:12:28.136 --> 02:12:32.698
carefully at the context, the relationship
and the opportunities they

02:12:32.699 --> 02:12:37.663
have been offered to contribute. Our
intention matters, and so does how the

02:12:37.664 --> 02:12:42.897
process is experienced. Where the same
barrier keeps appearing across families,

02:12:42.898 --> 02:12:48.063
ask what leadership could change in the
process itself. A recurring difficulty

02:12:48.064 --> 02:12:52.960
with timing, language or decision-making
deserves attention at that level.

02:12:54.480 --> 02:12:59.401
Let's explore that on page twenty-three,
under Power dynamics. You'll need three

02:12:59.402 --> 02:13:04.447
sticky notes, or three spaces on a page.
On the first, write an example of a power

02:13:04.448 --> 02:13:09.368
imbalance from a home perspective. On the
second, write an example from a school

02:13:09.369 --> 02:13:14.165
perspective. On the third, write how those
imbalances could be addressed. Keep

02:13:14.166 --> 02:13:18.588
the people involved anonymous. You might
think about language, access to

02:13:18.589 --> 02:13:23.323
information, who attends a meeting, or who
gets to define the problem. School

02:13:23.324 --> 02:13:28.369
acronyms such as NAPLAN, NCCD and IEP are
familiar to many educators, but we can't

02:13:28.370 --> 02:13:33.103
assume they are equally accessible to
families. Professional status, cultural

02:13:33.104 --> 02:13:37.464
expectations and the involvement of
external providers can also shape a

02:13:37.465 --> 02:13:42.012
meeting. Share your notes with your table.
If you would welcome a movement

02:13:42.013 --> 02:13:46.497
break, you could share with someone
elsewhere in the room, or place notes

02:13:46.498 --> 02:13:50.921
under Home, School and Solutions on a
wall. The live activity allows six

02:13:50.922 --> 02:13:55.593
minutes. Pause here to use that time. Look
for something that could make the

02:13:55.594 --> 02:13:58.333
process fairer, and resume when you're
ready.

02:14:06.360 --> 02:14:11.047
Let's come back together. Look at the
changes you've proposed through four

02:14:11.048 --> 02:14:15.478
questions. Who sets the agenda? Whose
knowledge is heard? Who can make

02:14:15.479 --> 02:14:19.845
decisions? What can the school change
about the process? Power can be

02:14:19.846 --> 02:14:24.340
structural, historical and cultural. It
can also operate without anyone

02:14:24.341 --> 02:14:29.478
consciously intending it. A difference in
status may feel significant even when a

02:14:29.479 --> 02:14:34.166
meeting has been organised with good
intentions. Choose one process change

02:14:34.167 --> 02:14:39.239
from your notes. It could be a change to
preparation, communication, the meeting

02:14:39.240 --> 02:14:44.441
itself or follow-up. Keep it beside your
student planning. We'll carry it into the

02:14:44.442 --> 02:14:49.322
bridge reflection at the end of this
session, and then into your conversation

02:14:49.323 --> 02:14:53.753
plan in Session Six. Which of your
proposed changes needs a leadership

02:14:53.754 --> 02:14:58.890
decision? Note that alongside it, so the
next step has a route through the school

02:14:58.891 --> 02:15:02.551
rather than resting entirely with one
meeting participant.

02:15:04.080 --> 02:15:09.447
We're going to hear an example involving
Adon, where decisions were made without

02:15:09.448 --> 02:15:14.408
the student being listened to or
consulted. As you watch, pay attention to

02:15:14.409 --> 02:15:19.436
what decisions were made, and who made
them. Then consider the impact those

02:15:19.437 --> 02:15:24.464
decisions had on Adon. There's space to
reflect under Student voice on page

02:15:24.465 --> 02:15:26.230
twenty-three. Let's watch.

02:17:18.680 --> 02:17:23.500
What impact did those decisions have on
Adon? Take a moment to consider what you

02:17:23.501 --> 02:17:28.382
heard, and what might have been different
if Adon's perspective had been included

02:17:28.383 --> 02:17:33.143
in the decisions. Now bring the question
back to the students you have in mind.

02:17:33.144 --> 02:17:37.842
How are their preferences asked for and
heard? Is there a way for a student to

02:17:37.843 --> 02:17:42.785
tell you that something isn't working, as
well as to choose between options adults

02:17:42.786 --> 02:17:47.240
have already offered? Pause here if you'd
like time to reflect or discuss.

02:17:55.240 --> 02:17:59.870
Students need a say in decisions about
them, in ways that work with their

02:17:59.871 --> 02:18:04.886
communication. Even a young child can
express a preference. We can offer visual

02:18:04.887 --> 02:18:10.160
choices, attend to a response, and honour
what it tells us. Questions such as, what

02:18:10.161 --> 02:18:15.112
helps, what's hard, and what would you
like teachers to know, can open up that

02:18:15.113 --> 02:18:20.257
understanding. We may need to change the
format, the setting or the time we allow

02:18:20.258 --> 02:18:24.887
for an answer. Where a student uses words,
record their words accurately.

02:18:24.888 --> 02:18:29.646
Where they communicate in other ways, make
clear what they communicated and

02:18:29.647 --> 02:18:34.277
how. Keep adult interpretation visible as
interpretation. Including their

02:18:34.278 --> 02:18:38.907
perspective in the record, and acting on
it, helps build a foundation for

02:18:38.908 --> 02:18:43.795
self-advocacy. Ask where your school
records that perspective, who can access

02:18:43.796 --> 02:18:48.490
it appropriately, and how it remains
available when decisions move between

02:18:48.491 --> 02:18:48.876
staff.

02:18:50.400 --> 02:18:55.525
Turn to the Partnership activity grid on
page twenty-four. This gives us a way to

02:18:55.526 --> 02:19:00.716
look at the people in a partnership with a
little more care. Work through the four

02:19:00.717 --> 02:19:05.457
partners: the student, parents and carers,
teachers, and paraprofessionals.

02:19:05.458 --> 02:19:10.391
For each, note what they bring and the
challenges they might face. Think about

02:19:10.392 --> 02:19:15.005
knowledge, relationships, time,
communication and the demands each person

02:19:15.006 --> 02:19:19.554
may be managing. Avoid assuming that every
person in a role has the same

02:19:19.555 --> 02:19:24.168
experience. Then compare your four
sections. What do they have in common?

02:19:24.169 --> 02:19:29.038
Where do you see different knowledge or
different needs? What could help them

02:19:29.039 --> 02:19:33.844
work together? Give yourselves eight
minutes for the full activity. Everyone

02:19:33.845 --> 02:19:38.906
can contribute in a way that suits them,
and you can write or reflect quietly as

02:19:38.907 --> 02:19:43.840
well as talk. Keep the student at the
centre of the grid. Pause the video now,

02:19:43.841 --> 02:19:45.570
and return for the debrief.

02:19:53.600 --> 02:19:58.236
Let's draw that together. Look across your
grid for similarities and

02:19:58.237 --> 02:20:03.358
differences. Which strengths might
complement one another? Which challenges

02:20:03.359 --> 02:20:08.410
could the partnership itself help address?
A useful next question is, what

02:20:08.411 --> 02:20:13.255
would make this partnership more
successful? Perhaps you need a clearer

02:20:13.256 --> 02:20:18.722
shared purpose, a different communication
arrangement, or a way for one person's

02:20:18.723 --> 02:20:24.121
knowledge to be heard earlier. Use the
circumstances you've identified to guide

02:20:24.122 --> 02:20:29.381
that choice. Return to our collaboration
principles: shared goals and values,

02:20:29.382 --> 02:20:34.156
trust and respect, consultation, curiosity
and student voice. Assuming

02:20:34.157 --> 02:20:38.724
good intent about outcomes for the student
can help us begin, while

02:20:38.725 --> 02:20:43.153
listening tells us what each person
actually needs and hopes for.

02:20:43.154 --> 02:20:48.621
Understanding each partner's strengths and
challenges gives trust and confidence

02:20:48.622 --> 02:20:50.905
something practical to grow from.

02:20:52.440 --> 02:20:57.492
Let's draw on relationships that already
work. Still on page twenty-four, find

02:20:57.493 --> 02:21:02.611
What works? In your school group, think of
a family with whom you have, or have

02:21:02.612 --> 02:21:07.599
had, a good relationship. How did you
establish it? What does it look like in

02:21:07.600 --> 02:21:12.324
practice? Why does it work? Try to
identify the actual moves that helped:

02:21:12.325 --> 02:21:17.443
what someone did before a meeting, how
information was shared, or how a concern

02:21:17.444 --> 02:21:22.758
was handled. Keep the example anonymous,
and stay with what you can learn from the

02:21:22.759 --> 02:21:27.418
relationship. If you're working alone,
make a few notes against the same

02:21:27.419 --> 02:21:31.815
questions. The activity allows six
minutes. Pause the video for that

02:21:31.816 --> 02:21:36.343
reflection and conversation. When you
return, we'll connect it to some

02:21:36.344 --> 02:21:39.296
practices that can help everyone at the
table.

02:21:47.320 --> 02:21:52.277
Let's come back. Choose one idea from your
reflection that you could carry into

02:21:52.278 --> 02:21:57.045
another relationship. The list here gives
us some practical starting points:

02:21:57.046 --> 02:22:01.559
prepare, share information, ask questions,
seek clarification and assume

02:22:01.560 --> 02:22:06.772
the best. On the school side, preparation
includes sending the agenda beforehand so

02:22:06.773 --> 02:22:11.539
the family knows what to expect, and
inviting their input. Welcome a support

02:22:11.540 --> 02:22:16.243
person. That can make it easier for
someone to contribute and keep track of

02:22:16.244 --> 02:22:20.884
what is being discussed. Open with a
strength. Share notes or minutes, and

02:22:20.885 --> 02:22:25.906
follow up in writing so people can check
what was agreed. An opening such as, as

02:22:25.907 --> 02:22:30.356
we agreed, is only useful when the record
actually reflects that shared

02:22:30.357 --> 02:22:35.315
understanding. When a meeting has gone
badly, acknowledging that experience and

02:22:35.316 --> 02:22:40.209
offering a reset can help. Ask what would
make the next meeting fairer or more

02:22:40.210 --> 02:22:44.659
accessible. Families continue to support
learning across many settings.

02:22:44.660 --> 02:22:49.173
Recognising their knowledge and
contribution means making room for it to

02:22:49.174 --> 02:22:51.080
shape the work we do together.

02:22:52.600 --> 02:22:57.902
Meeting families where they are begins
with listening before solving. Ask about

02:22:57.903 --> 02:23:03.408
communication preferences and priorities.
Make room for questions and uncertainty,

02:23:03.409 --> 02:23:08.099
including questions we haven't
anticipated. Explain roles, options and

02:23:08.100 --> 02:23:13.402
next steps in plain language. Check what
is workable for the family now. A plan

02:23:13.403 --> 02:23:18.228
that looks simple within a school
timetable may interact with demands we

02:23:18.229 --> 02:23:23.735
don't know about. Avoid judging the pace
of a response, the choices someone makes,

02:23:23.736 --> 02:23:28.970
or the time they need to consider an
option. This brings our environment-first

02:23:28.971 --> 02:23:34.204
thinking into the meeting itself. Where
might the school's language, timing or

02:23:34.205 --> 02:23:39.575
process create a barrier? What could we
change? A different format, questions in

02:23:39.576 --> 02:23:44.606
advance, a quieter place, or clearer
follow-up may change the experience of

02:23:44.607 --> 02:23:50.044
consultation. Keep asking what you need to
understand. Listening and clarity give

02:23:50.045 --> 02:23:55.483
us a stronger basis for solving something
together. Where the same access barrier

02:23:55.484 --> 02:24:00.785
comes up for several families, bring it
back to the school process. Leaders can

02:24:00.786 --> 02:24:06.020
review the shared arrangements as well as
respond to one family’s preferences.

02:24:07.560 --> 02:24:11.791
On page twenty-five you'll find the
Consultation checklist. You can use it

02:24:11.792 --> 02:24:15.966
before a meeting, and return to it
afterwards. Start with a clear, shared

02:24:15.967 --> 02:24:20.546
purpose. What are we trying to understand
or decide together? Use open questions

02:24:20.547 --> 02:24:24.952
that allow an answer you haven't already
chosen. Provide more than one way to

02:24:24.953 --> 02:24:29.706
contribute, and respect the knowledge the
student and family bring. Record what you

02:24:29.707 --> 02:24:33.707
hear accurately. It can help to check your
understanding aloud: have I

02:24:33.708 --> 02:24:38.229
understood that correctly? Then agree an
action, who is responsible and how you

02:24:38.230 --> 02:24:42.113
will follow up. If something remains
undecided, make that clear too.

02:24:42.114 --> 02:24:46.346
Afterwards, ask yourself whether the
consultation changed or extended your

02:24:46.347 --> 02:24:50.868
understanding. Could everyone contribute
in a workable way? Do people know what

02:24:50.869 --> 02:24:55.506
happens next? Ask, listen and follow up. A
checklist or planning tool can help us

02:24:55.507 --> 02:25:00.086
remember those steps, while the quality of
the consultation still depends on our

02:25:00.087 --> 02:25:04.724
attention to the people involved. Now use
the same checklist with your own staff.

02:25:04.725 --> 02:25:09.246
Support staff may have a close picture of
a student’s day, and teachers may see

02:25:09.247 --> 02:25:13.421
where the timetable or routines are
hardest to manage. How does what they

02:25:13.422 --> 02:25:18.001
notice reach decisions in your school? Ask
the leadership team: what evidence do

02:25:18.002 --> 02:25:22.233
we expect, what enables consistent
adjustments, and what is our particular

02:25:22.234 --> 02:25:26.697
responsibility? Make room for more than
one way to contribute. Then tell staff

02:25:26.698 --> 02:25:30.408
what was heard and what changed, just as
you would with families.

02:25:31.920 --> 02:25:36.844
Let's look first at consulting students.
Begin by presuming competence, and make

02:25:36.845 --> 02:25:41.332
communication accessible. That might
involve augmentative and alternative

02:25:41.333 --> 02:25:46.194
communication, visuals, writing, drawing,
video or symbols. Offer questions and

02:25:46.195 --> 02:25:50.558
meeting information beforehand, simple
choices where useful, and enough

02:25:50.559 --> 02:25:54.921
processing time. Notice the sensory
environment. Build on the student's

02:25:54.922 --> 02:25:59.846
strengths and interests. Behaviour may
communicate preferences or difficulty, so

02:25:59.847 --> 02:26:04.396
observe carefully and check your
interpretation wherever possible. Trusted

02:26:04.397 --> 02:26:09.259
supporters can assist communication while
keeping the student's own perspective

02:26:09.260 --> 02:26:14.059
central. What a student tells us needs to
influence what we do. With families,

02:26:14.060 --> 02:26:18.796
begin with the student's strengths,
interests and what works. Listen to their

02:26:18.797 --> 02:26:23.160
knowledge without assuming you already
know their experience. Use plain

02:26:23.161 --> 02:26:27.399
language, explain school terms and respect
differences in culture and

02:26:27.400 --> 02:26:31.575
perspective. Some parents may be
neurodivergent themselves, so check

02:26:31.576 --> 02:26:36.188
communication preferences rather than
making assumptions. Use real examples

02:26:36.189 --> 02:26:40.863
from school to discuss needs, goals,
participation and possible adjustments.

02:26:40.864 --> 02:26:45.663
Check shared understanding, agree next
steps and keep the conversation open as

02:26:45.664 --> 02:26:50.152
circumstances change. This extends the
consultation work we introduced in

02:26:50.153 --> 02:26:54.952
Session Four. Choose one practice from
each column that the school could build

02:26:54.953 --> 02:26:59.939
into its usual meeting processes, keeping
the student and family you have in mind

02:26:59.940 --> 02:27:02.245
visible. Pause to note those choices.

02:27:10.280 --> 02:27:14.741
As you look at your two choices, check
what they would make easier for the

02:27:14.742 --> 02:27:18.959
person involved. Can the student
communicate more comfortably? Can the

02:27:18.960 --> 02:27:23.726
family prepare, ask a question or add
knowledge that might otherwise be missed?

02:27:23.727 --> 02:27:28.616
Keep those choices with your conversation
notes. Consultation becomes more useful

02:27:28.617 --> 02:27:32.895
when we adapt it to the people involved,
and then act on what we learn.

02:27:34.400 --> 02:27:39.249
Partnership becomes particularly important
when a student's capacity runs

02:27:39.250 --> 02:27:43.965
out. The terms on this slide are meltdown,
shutdown, and what the slide

02:27:43.966 --> 02:27:49.354
calls School Can't. That is, the student
cannot attend school. We are considering

02:27:49.355 --> 02:27:54.406
these through capacity, accumulated
demands and the parts of experience that

02:27:54.407 --> 02:27:59.526
may be difficult for others to see. These
experiences need a response without

02:27:59.527 --> 02:28:03.837
blame. Describing them as wilful
misbehaviour, or as a failure of

02:28:03.838 --> 02:28:08.350
parenting, can close down the
understanding we need. Home and school

02:28:08.351 --> 02:28:13.133
may see different parts of the student's
day. A difference between those

02:28:13.134 --> 02:28:18.455
observations is a reason to listen and
learn more. Bring back the questions from

02:28:18.456 --> 02:28:23.642
Sessions Two and Three. What has the day
been asking of this student? What may

02:28:23.643 --> 02:28:28.829
have accumulated? What information does
the student have, and what might their

02:28:28.830 --> 02:28:33.545
family know about recovery? Keep the focus
on safety, what preceded the

02:28:33.546 --> 02:28:38.799
situation, what supports recovery, and
what can change in the environment, task

02:28:38.800 --> 02:28:43.649
or response. Use the workshop's safety
guidance and the support processes

02:28:43.650 --> 02:28:48.634
relevant to your setting. The partnership
task here is to share information

02:28:48.635 --> 02:28:53.619
respectfully, listen carefully and
identify changes that may reduce demands

02:28:53.620 --> 02:28:58.806
and improve the student's experience. As
leaders, check whether relevant staff

02:28:58.807 --> 02:29:04.060
know your school’s wellbeing, safety and
support pathways. Make the local route

02:29:04.061 --> 02:29:08.439
clear, including where staff take a
concern and who follows it up.

02:29:09.960 --> 02:29:14.888
Here are two Positive Partnerships tools
that can help make those conversations

02:29:14.889 --> 02:29:19.627
concrete. On the left, the Working
Together Communication Tool asks how home

02:29:19.628 --> 02:29:24.555
and school will communicate, how often,
about what, and what the next steps and

02:29:24.556 --> 02:29:29.295
dates will be. You can discuss options
such as text, email, an app, phone, a

02:29:29.296 --> 02:29:33.907
communication book or face-to-face
conversation. Choose what is useful and

02:29:33.908 --> 02:29:38.646
workable together. On the right, the
Partnership Planning Template asks what

02:29:38.647 --> 02:29:43.638
has worked, what has been challenging and
what could help. It gives examples and

02:29:43.639 --> 02:29:48.314
space for your own ideas. You might use it
to explore meeting arrangements,

02:29:48.315 --> 02:29:53.495
feeling heard, communication or including
the young person's voice. For example, if

02:29:53.496 --> 02:29:58.171
a family says phone calls are hard to
manage during work, you could use the

02:29:58.172 --> 02:30:03.037
tools to discuss another option and agree
when it will be reviewed. That is an

02:30:03.038 --> 02:30:07.776
illustrative use of the prompts; the
family may suggest something different.

02:30:07.777 --> 02:30:12.641
Use the tools with people. Leave room for
their priorities to change what gets

02:30:12.642 --> 02:30:17.570
written. A completed page is useful when
it records a shared understanding that

02:30:17.571 --> 02:30:22.372
can guide the next conversation. Consider
where these tools fit your school’s

02:30:22.373 --> 02:30:27.048
existing meeting, decision and review
processes, so the record continues to

02:30:27.049 --> 02:30:28.628
inform what happens next.

02:30:30.160 --> 02:30:35.605
The NCCD Meetings Toolkit is another
practical resource. NCCD stands for the

02:30:35.606 --> 02:30:41.341
Nationally Consistent Collection of Data
on School Students with Disability. You

02:30:41.342 --> 02:30:47.223
can find the toolkit on the official NCCD
website. Its sections support requesting

02:30:47.224 --> 02:30:52.741
and managing meetings, being prepared,
managing emotions, keeping records and

02:30:52.742 --> 02:30:58.114
considering whether a meeting was
successful. Use it to make the purpose of

02:30:58.115 --> 02:31:03.560
a meeting, the consultation process,
possible adjustments, evidence and next

02:31:03.561 --> 02:31:09.369
steps clear. Explain responsibilities and
follow-up in a way that the student and

02:31:09.370 --> 02:31:14.888
family can understand. People need to know
why a meeting is happening, who is

02:31:14.889 --> 02:31:20.479
involved and how they can contribute.
Follow the processes used in your school

02:31:20.480 --> 02:31:25.852
or system, and use current official NCCD
information when you need specific

02:31:25.853 --> 02:31:31.370
guidance. The toolkit gives you a way to
prepare and review the process while

02:31:31.371 --> 02:31:37.107
keeping the discussion connected to the
student's experience. Check who uses the

02:31:37.108 --> 02:31:41.899
toolkit in your school, who makes
decisions, how student and family

02:31:41.900 --> 02:31:47.708
knowledge enters the process and what is
recorded. Those responsibilities need to

02:31:47.709 --> 02:31:50.685
be understandable to the people involved.

02:31:52.200 --> 02:31:57.664
There are also practical support pathways
beyond today's workshop. Page thirty of

02:31:57.665 --> 02:32:02.719
your Participant Guide brings the
resources together. Positive Partnerships

02:32:02.720 --> 02:32:07.978
provides resources and learning options
for educators and families. This slide

02:32:07.979 --> 02:32:13.375
also shows Autism Connect, the national
autism information helpline, with phone,

02:32:13.376 --> 02:32:18.634
email and webchat options. Disability
Gateway can help people with disability,

02:32:18.635 --> 02:32:24.099
families and carers find information and
services. Carer Gateway provides support

02:32:24.100 --> 02:32:29.427
for carers. The contact details are on the
slide, so pause if you would like to

02:32:29.428 --> 02:32:34.550
record them. Choose a pathway that fits
the person's question, and check its

02:32:34.551 --> 02:32:39.810
website for the available support. We can
make families aware of these options

02:32:39.811 --> 02:32:42.679
while listening to what they want and
need.

02:32:45.680 --> 02:32:50.438
Before we finish this session, let's
prepare the bridge to one purposeful

02:32:50.439 --> 02:32:55.528
conversation. Return to the student you
have in mind and the school change you

02:32:55.529 --> 02:33:00.419
developed in Session Four. Open page
twenty-five, Bridge to the next family

02:33:00.420 --> 02:33:04.980
conversation. Work through these questions
privately. What needs to be

02:33:04.981 --> 02:33:09.937
understood? What question will open the
conversation? How will the student's

02:33:09.938 --> 02:33:14.961
voice be included? What can be decided
together? How and when will the school

02:33:14.962 --> 02:33:19.786
follow up? Bring in the process change you
chose during the power-dynamics

02:33:19.787 --> 02:33:24.677
activity. Make it part of how the
conversation will happen, as well as what

02:33:24.678 --> 02:33:30.031
you will ask. You are preparing your own
purpose and approach. Leave space for the

02:33:30.032 --> 02:33:34.856
student's and family's knowledge to change
your understanding and the next

02:33:34.857 --> 02:33:40.012
step. Keep names out of your workshop
notes. Allow about four minutes to write.

02:33:40.013 --> 02:33:44.705
Pause the video now, and resume when
you're ready to close this session.

02:33:52.720 --> 02:33:57.418
Take a final look at your opening
question. Does it invite the other

02:33:57.419 --> 02:34:02.398
person’s knowledge? Have you left
something meaningful to understand and

02:34:02.399 --> 02:34:08.149
decide together? Keep these notes for the
conversation plan on page twenty-eight in

02:34:08.150 --> 02:34:13.619
Session Six. Keep the colleague you named
under I will share it with in view as

02:34:13.620 --> 02:34:18.669
well. One school change and one purposeful
conversation will connect your

02:34:18.670 --> 02:34:23.859
learning with the people and systems you
lead. We’ll bring those next steps

02:34:23.860 --> 02:34:26.945
together through belonging, capacity and
joy.

02:34:33.480 --> 02:34:38.229
Welcome to our final session, Flourishing
and Full Circle. We're going to return to

02:34:38.230 --> 02:34:42.632
the students and questions you began with,
and turn your thinking into a next

02:34:42.633 --> 02:34:47.150
step you can use. Have your My students
card and your partnership notes nearby.

02:34:47.151 --> 02:34:51.495
By the end of this session, you'll have
one school change and one purposeful

02:34:51.496 --> 02:34:56.013
conversation with a family or student to
take forward. We'll also make time for

02:34:56.014 --> 02:35:00.590
reflection, resources and feedback. You
don't need to carry away an unmanageable

02:35:00.591 --> 02:35:05.166
list. One thoughtful action, informed by
the people involved and open to review,

02:35:05.167 --> 02:35:06.730
is a useful place to begin.

02:35:08.240 --> 02:35:13.592
Belonging, capacity and joy bring the
threads of today together. Keep all three

02:35:13.593 --> 02:35:19.014
in the picture when you consider whether a
change is helping a student flourish.

02:35:19.015 --> 02:35:23.681
Belonging means being recognised, included
and able to participate as

02:35:23.682 --> 02:35:28.966
oneself. Capacity asks us to consider what
the day costs, what it protects and

02:35:28.967 --> 02:35:34.044
what helps restore energy. Joy includes
interests, connection and authentic

02:35:34.045 --> 02:35:39.260
expression. These ideas connect our
neurodiversity-affirming lens, our school

02:35:39.261 --> 02:35:44.133
design and our partnerships. We begin with
the person, learn about their

02:35:44.134 --> 02:35:49.761
experience, and bring student, family and
school knowledge into the decisions. Look

02:35:49.762 --> 02:35:55.183
again at the design change you chose in
Session Four. It may reduce a barrier to

02:35:55.184 --> 02:36:00.193
participation. Could it also support
belonging or joy? How might it affect

02:36:00.194 --> 02:36:04.860
what the day asks of the student? Remember
the questions from Session

02:36:04.861 --> 02:36:09.870
Three: what costs, and what lightens?
Academic progress can be part of the

02:36:09.871 --> 02:36:15.017
picture, but it won't tell us everything
about a student's experience. Quiet

02:36:15.018 --> 02:36:20.164
compliance or doing something without
support is not, by itself, evidence of

02:36:20.165 --> 02:36:25.175
flourishing. Keep returning to the
student, and to belonging, capacity and

02:36:25.176 --> 02:36:28.812
joy, as you judge what your change is
making possible.

02:36:30.320 --> 02:36:35.358
Let's bring the strongest ideas together
at your table. Open page twenty-six of

02:36:35.359 --> 02:36:40.268
your Participant Guide. Each person has
about a minute to share the strongest

02:36:40.269 --> 02:36:44.984
thing they're taking back to school.
Choose something that has stayed with

02:36:44.985 --> 02:36:49.442
you: an insight, a question or a practical
change. You can draw on the

02:36:49.443 --> 02:36:54.481
gallery walk from Session Four as well as
your own planning. Then make a shared

02:36:54.482 --> 02:36:59.520
shortlist of three ideas from the table
that are most worth spreading. For each

02:36:59.521 --> 02:37:04.624
idea, name who needs to hear it, and the
meeting or moment where you could share

02:37:04.625 --> 02:37:09.082
it. Be as concrete as you can. A
particular colleague and a particular

02:37:09.083 --> 02:37:14.185
opportunity will help you see what to do
next. The activity allows eight minutes

02:37:14.186 --> 02:37:18.514
altogether. If you're working
independently, make your own shortlist

02:37:18.515 --> 02:37:23.230
and identify who you could discuss it
with. Pause here to work through the

02:37:23.231 --> 02:37:25.814
activity, then resume for the next step.

02:37:33.840 --> 02:37:38.457
Let's return to your shortlist. Check that
each idea has somewhere to go: a

02:37:38.458 --> 02:37:43.200
person who needs to hear it, and a real
opportunity to talk. If an idea still

02:37:43.201 --> 02:37:48.129
feels broad, add one example of what it
could mean in practice. You can keep the

02:37:48.130 --> 02:37:52.560
other ideas for later. For now, we're
going to make your own next action

02:37:52.561 --> 02:37:55.805
specific enough to begin, and clear enough
to review.

02:37:57.320 --> 02:38:02.239
Turn to page twenty-seven, Start,
Continue, Reconsider. Place your Session

02:38:02.240 --> 02:38:06.957
Four Commit notes and the school change on
page twenty beside it. Think

02:38:06.958 --> 02:38:11.944
school-wide. Your Start can be the
this-term step you have already planned.

02:38:11.945 --> 02:38:17.268
Under Start, choose a small strategy you
can begin next week. It can be the same

02:38:17.269 --> 02:38:21.918
design change you've already been
developing. Under Continue, identify

02:38:21.919 --> 02:38:26.973
something today has confirmed you already
do well. Under Reconsider, name an

02:38:26.974 --> 02:38:31.623
approach you would now look at
differently. For each, ask how you will

02:38:31.624 --> 02:38:36.611
know it worked, and what evidence will
help you judge that. For your Start,

02:38:36.612 --> 02:38:41.935
these sentence stems may help: I will. The
barrier or demand I am addressing is.

02:38:41.936 --> 02:38:47.259
I will notice. I need to consult. I will
review this on. Choose something within

02:38:47.260 --> 02:38:52.112
your influence. Keep the focus on the
student's access, participation and

02:38:52.113 --> 02:38:57.369
experience. An action that depends on the
student appearing less Autistic needs

02:38:57.370 --> 02:39:02.828
rethinking. Consider what you can change
in the environment, task or response, and

02:39:02.829 --> 02:39:08.219
whose knowledge you need before or while
you try it. Allow eight minutes. This is

02:39:08.220 --> 02:39:13.611
your card. Choose one thing to share with
your executive team. You decide what to

02:39:13.612 --> 02:39:18.396
share from your card. Pause the video now,
and we'll return to check the

02:39:18.397 --> 02:39:18.868
action.

02:39:26.880 --> 02:39:32.134
Let's look once more at your Start. Is it
small enough to begin? Does it name a

02:39:32.135 --> 02:39:37.254
barrier or demand you can act on? Have you
identified when you'll consult the

02:39:37.255 --> 02:39:42.576
student, family or team, and when you'll
review what happens? Check the evidence

02:39:42.577 --> 02:39:47.696
you plan to notice. If the measure is
simply that the student becomes quieter

02:39:47.697 --> 02:39:52.546
or complies more readily, look again. What
will help you understand their

02:39:52.547 --> 02:39:57.667
access, participation and experience?
Include their perspective in a way that

02:39:57.668 --> 02:40:02.584
works for them. Keep the action small,
testable and open to revision. Your

02:40:02.585 --> 02:40:07.502
Continue and Reconsider are useful
alongside it: one recognises a practice

02:40:07.503 --> 02:40:11.275
worth sustaining, and the other keeps your
learning open.

02:40:12.800 --> 02:40:18.191
Now let's plan the next conversation, on
page twenty-eight. Bring forward your

02:40:18.192 --> 02:40:23.372
Session Five bridge notes from page
twenty-five, and the process change you

02:40:23.373 --> 02:40:28.624
chose in the power-dynamics reflection on
page twenty-three. Choose one real

02:40:28.625 --> 02:40:33.805
conversation you can have within two
weeks, with a student's family or with

02:40:33.806 --> 02:40:39.407
the student themselves. Begin by writing
the purpose in one sentence. What do you

02:40:39.408 --> 02:40:44.869
need to understand? Leave room for their
perspective to shape the conversation.

02:40:44.870 --> 02:40:50.400
Plan what you'll share, beginning with a
strength, what you'll ask specifically,

02:40:50.401 --> 02:40:55.792
and what you'll offer. Include how the
student's communication and preferences

02:40:55.793 --> 02:41:01.254
will be heard. Write two open questions,
and note what you will listen for. Now

02:41:01.255 --> 02:41:06.645
write your opening sentence, word for
word. Say it quietly to yourself if that

02:41:06.646 --> 02:41:11.897
helps you hear how it might land. Is it
clear and respectful? Does it invite

02:41:11.898 --> 02:41:17.219
something you don't already know? Name one
possible shared next step, leaving

02:41:17.220 --> 02:41:22.610
room for the conversation to change it.
Record how you'll follow up, and when,

02:41:22.611 --> 02:41:27.582
where and how the conversation will
happen. Apply your process change to

02:41:27.583 --> 02:41:33.113
those arrangements. Use initials or roles
in your workshop notes. You have eight

02:41:33.114 --> 02:41:38.155
minutes for the full activity. Pause here
to make your plan, then resume.

02:41:46.160 --> 02:41:51.244
Take a final look at your conversation
plan. Have you prepared to listen, as

02:41:51.245 --> 02:41:56.465
well as to speak? Does the plan leave
something meaningful to decide together?

02:41:56.466 --> 02:42:01.619
Have you made a way for the student's own
perspective to be included? You can

02:42:01.620 --> 02:42:06.704
prepare your opening and your questions
without scripting the other person's

02:42:06.705 --> 02:42:11.315
response. If new knowledge changes the
next step you had in mind, the

02:42:11.316 --> 02:42:16.536
conversation is doing useful work. Keep
the time and follow-up visible in your

02:42:16.537 --> 02:42:21.079
plan. One school action and one
conversation now connect what you've

02:42:21.080 --> 02:42:25.215
learned today to the people and setting
where you will use it.

02:42:26.720 --> 02:42:31.476
This slide is the place for Caitlin's
account of how teacher belief shaped

02:42:31.477 --> 02:42:36.560
their success. That video hasn't been
supplied in this version of the workshop,

02:42:36.561 --> 02:42:41.252
so we'll move directly to the final
reflection. Carry its focus with you:

02:42:41.253 --> 02:42:46.270
teacher belief, student experience, and
the difference our responses can make.

02:42:47.800 --> 02:42:52.657
Return to page five of your Participant
Guide, My starting point. Earlier, you

02:42:52.658 --> 02:42:57.451
wrote about what school is for and what
you were curious about. Read your own

02:42:57.452 --> 02:43:01.867
words back. Now turn to the final
reflection on page twenty-nine. Where

02:43:01.868 --> 02:43:06.409
were you when we began? Where are you now?
What's different in how you're

02:43:06.410 --> 02:43:11.456
thinking about the students you have in
mind? Take a little quiet time with those

02:43:11.457 --> 02:43:15.872
questions. Then write one sentence, just
for yourself: what will you do

02:43:15.873 --> 02:43:20.667
differently in your leadership? This
reflection can stay private. There is no

02:43:20.668 --> 02:43:25.461
need to share it. The live workshop allows
four minutes here. Pause the video

02:43:25.462 --> 02:43:28.805
for the time you need, then resume when
you are ready.

02:43:36.840 --> 02:43:41.175
Keep that sentence with your action card.
It may capture a shift you want to

02:43:41.176 --> 02:43:45.799
remember when you're back in the pace of a
school day. You can return to it after

02:43:45.800 --> 02:43:50.019
you've tried your change and had your
conversation, and notice what you've

02:43:50.020 --> 02:43:50.770
learned then.

02:43:52.280 --> 02:43:57.612
What are you curious to learn more about
next? Look back at your questions on page

02:43:57.613 --> 02:44:02.814
five. Which have been answered, and which
have become more specific? Page thirty

02:44:02.815 --> 02:44:07.554
brings together pathways for continuing
the learning. Webinars and deeper

02:44:07.555 --> 02:44:12.427
sessions can help you explore topics such
as Universal Design for Learning,

02:44:12.428 --> 02:44:17.826
executive functioning, sensory design and
anxiety. The Online Community of Practice

02:44:17.827 --> 02:44:22.566
is intended as a place to keep talking
with other educators. The Positive

02:44:22.567 --> 02:44:27.241
Partnerships website also provides
resources for educators, families and

02:44:27.242 --> 02:44:32.443
students. The particular webinar titles
and dates, and the Community of Practice

02:44:32.444 --> 02:44:37.315
link, are still awaiting confirmation in
these materials. Use the confirmed

02:44:37.316 --> 02:44:42.056
follow-up information from your workshop
organiser when those details are

02:44:42.057 --> 02:44:46.928
available. The workshop plan also includes
sharing photographs of wall work

02:44:46.929 --> 02:44:51.932
through that community; check the local
arrangements for your group. For now,

02:44:51.933 --> 02:44:56.541
choose the pathway that fits your next
question. You can begin with the

02:44:56.542 --> 02:45:01.742
resources at positive partnerships dot com
dot au. Today is a starting point for

02:45:01.743 --> 02:45:04.771
continued learning, reflection and
partnership.

02:45:06.280 --> 02:45:11.135
Here are the Positive Partnerships website
and social channels. You can note

02:45:11.136 --> 02:45:16.315
the details, take a screenshot, or return
to this slide later. The slide includes

02:45:16.316 --> 02:45:21.237
Facebook, Instagram, Threads, LinkedIn and
Pinterest, as well as the website.

02:45:21.238 --> 02:45:26.222
Choose the way of staying connected that
works for you. Pause the video if you

02:45:26.223 --> 02:45:28.941
would like longer with the contact
details.

02:45:31.960 --> 02:45:36.853
Before we close, there is time for
feedback. Your reflections can help the

02:45:36.854 --> 02:45:41.680
team understand what was useful, what
needs clarification, and what could

02:45:41.681 --> 02:45:46.775
improve the workshop experience. The
feedback link is on this slide, with the

02:45:46.776 --> 02:45:51.669
code you can scan. If that way of
accessing the form doesn't work for you,

02:45:51.670 --> 02:45:56.966
let your workshop organiser know so you
can discuss an accessible way to provide

02:45:56.967 --> 02:46:02.329
your feedback. The live workshop protects
four minutes for this. If your group is

02:46:02.330 --> 02:46:07.290
using the form, pause the video now and
take that time. Then resume for our

02:46:07.291 --> 02:46:08.229
closing words.

02:46:16.240 --> 02:46:20.959
Thank you for taking the time to reflect
on the workshop. Keep any questions you

02:46:20.960 --> 02:46:25.679
still want to explore alongside your next
steps, so they can inform the learning

02:46:25.680 --> 02:46:27.291
that continues after today.

02:46:28.800 --> 02:46:33.089
Thank you for your attention, questions
and professional reflection throughout

02:46:33.090 --> 02:46:37.491
these sessions. Take your action card and
conversation plan with you, along with

02:46:37.492 --> 02:46:41.391
the sentence you wrote in your final
reflection. Stay curious, and keep

02:46:41.392 --> 02:46:45.402
students at the centre. Take one
thoughtful next step, include the people

02:46:45.403 --> 02:46:49.804
who hold relevant knowledge, and review
what happens. Student voice, observation

02:46:49.805 --> 02:46:54.094
and partnership can help you refine a
small change into something that makes a

02:46:54.095 --> 02:46:58.552
